(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["design",{"l:design/how-do-you-design-a-data-structure-from-the-oper":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Start from the interface, not from a structure. List every operation with its target cost, then give each operation that must be fast its own index: a dict for \"find by key\", a list for \"pick by position\", a heap or linked list for \"the next one in some order\". Write one invariant that says how the indexes agree, make every method restore it, and test against a slow model.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Think of a small library. The catalogue finds a book by title, the shelves keep books in call-number order so you can browse a range, and the returns trolley holds the most recently returned books on top. Same books, three indexes, each answering a different question quickly. The librarian's real job is bookkeeping: when a book moves, every index that mentions it must be updated, or the catalogue sends readers to an empty shelf.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Data structure design problems are exactly this. No single container answers every question fast, so you keep several, each specialised, and the difficulty moves from algorithms to keeping them consistent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Step 1: write the operations table\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Take a transit system that records check-ins and check-outs and reports the average trip time between two stations (LeetCode 1396). Before choosing anything, write what each operation must find and how fast:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Operation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Must find quickly\u003c/th>\u003cth>Target\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>check_in(rider, station, t)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>nothing: record where and when the rider entered\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>check_out(rider, station, t)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the rider's open trip\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>average(start, end)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the total time and trip count for that pair\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch5>Step 2: store only what the answers need\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The average needs a sum and a count per station pair, not the list of trips. An open trip needs only (station, time) per rider. So two dicts: \u003ccode>open_trips: rider -&gt; (station, t)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>totals: (start, end) -&gt; [total_time, trips]\u003c/code>. Storing every finished trip would also work, but \u003ccode>average\u003c/code> would then cost O(trips) unless you added a running sum anyway.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Step 3: write the invariant\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ci>At every moment, \u003ccode>open_trips\u003c/code> holds exactly the riders who have checked in and not out, and \u003ccode>totals[(a, b)]\u003c/code> holds the sum and count of every finished trip from a to b.\u003c/i> Each method must leave this sentence true.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Step 4: implement each method to restore the invariant\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>check_in\u003c/code> adds one entry; \u003ccode>check_out\u003c/code> removes the rider's entry and adds one trip to one pair; \u003ccode>average\u003c/code> reads. Each touches a constant number of dict entries.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The toolbox\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>You need\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cost in CPython\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Find, insert or delete by key\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>dict\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>set\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) average\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Pick by position, a uniformly random element\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>list\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) index; O(1) amortised append and pop at the end\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>First in, first out\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>collections.deque\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) at both ends\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Repeatedly take the smallest\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>heapq\u003c/code> on a list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n) push and pop\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sorted order, floor and ceiling\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sorted list plus \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n) search, O(n) insert\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Move an item to the end, given a handle to it\u003c/td>\u003ctd>doubly linked list, \u003ccode>OrderedDict.move_to_end\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every design in this module is two or three rows of this table glued together by an invariant.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each \u003ccode>UndergroundSystem\u003c/code> method performs at most two dict lookups and one insert or delete, each O(1) on average, so every operation is \u003cb>O(1) average\u003c/b>. Space is O(R + P): R riders currently inside the system plus P distinct station pairs ever travelled, independent of how many trips were taken.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For LeetCode 705 (a hash set without built-in hash tables) with m buckets and n keys, a hash that spreads keys evenly gives chains of expected length n/m, so \u003ccode>add\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>remove\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>contains\u003c/code> cost O(1 + n/m) expected. With keys limited to 0..10^6 you can also use a \u003ccode>bytearray\u003c/code> of 10^6 + 1 flags: 1 MB and O(1) worst case, but only because the key range is small.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reach for this process whenever a statement says \"design\", \"implement a class\" or lists several methods with costs. The cue is two different access patterns on the same data (by key and by order), which no single container serves.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Decide early what to keep. Aggregates (sum and count) are small and fast but can only answer the questions you planned for: from a sum and a count you can never recover the median trip time. Raw events answer future questions but cost memory and query time.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Keep\u003c/th>\u003cth>Memory\u003c/th>\u003cth>\u003ccode>average\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Can later answer the median?\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sum and count per pair\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(P)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Every trip, per pair\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(trips)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) with a running sum\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, by sorting or two heaps\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not hand-roll a hash set in production code; \u003ccode>set\u003c/code> is faster and better tested. Problems like 705 forbid built-ins precisely so that you practise the mechanics.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Two riders travel from Oak to Elm. Watch both dicts change:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">check_in(7, \"Oak\", 2): rider 7 opens a trip\u003c/p>\u003cpre>open_trips  \u003cmark>7: (\"Oak\", 2)\u003c/mark>\ntotals      {}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">check_in(9, \"Oak\", 4): a second open trip; nothing is finished yet\u003c/p>\u003cpre>open_trips  7: (\"Oak\", 2)   \u003cmark>9: (\"Oak\", 4)\u003c/mark>\ntotals      {}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">check_out(7, \"Elm\", 12): pop rider 7, add 12 - 2 = 10 to the Oak-Elm pair\u003c/p>\u003cpre>open_trips  9: (\"Oak\", 4)\ntotals      \u003cmark>(\"Oak\", \"Elm\"): [10, 1]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">check_out(9, \"Elm\", 18): pop rider 9, add 18 - 4 = 14\u003c/p>\u003cpre>open_trips  {}\ntotals      (\"Oak\", \"Elm\"): \u003cmark>[24, 2]\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">average(\"Oak\", \"Elm\") reads one entry: 24 / 2 = 12.0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>totals      (\"Oak\", \"Elm\"): [\u003cmark>24\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>]   -&gt;  12.0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Write the operations and the invariant in the docstring before any method; then each method is a few lines. LeetCode names the methods \u003ccode>checkIn\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>checkOut\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>getAverageTime\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class UndergroundSystem:\n    \"\"\"Average trip time between two stations.\n\n    Invariants: open_trips holds exactly the riders who checked in and have not checked out;\n    totals[(a, b)] == [sum of finished trip times from a to b, number of those trips].\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.open_trips = {}   # rider -&gt; (start station, check-in time)\n        self.totals = {}       # (start, end) -&gt; [total time, trips]\n\n    def check_in(self, rider, station, t):\n        self.open_trips[rider] = (station, t)\n\n    def check_out(self, rider, station, t):\n        start, t0 = self.open_trips.pop(rider)\n        rec = self.totals.setdefault((start, station), [0, 0])\n        rec[0] += t - t0\n        rec[1] += 1\n\n    def average(self, start, end):\n        total, trips = self.totals[(start, end)]\n        return total / trips\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The same habit applied to a hash set with chaining (bucket i holds the keys with \u003ccode>key % size == i\u003c/code>):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class MyHashSet:\n    def __init__(self, size=1024):\n        self.size = size\n        self.buckets = [[] for _ in range(size)]\n\n    def add(self, key):\n        b = self.buckets[key % self.size]\n        if key not in b:\n            b.append(key)\n\n    def remove(self, key):\n        b = self.buckets[key % self.size]\n        if key in b:\n            b.remove(key)\n\n    def contains(self, key):\n        return key in self.buckets[key % self.size]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Storing too much.\u003c/b> Keeping every trip makes \u003ccode>average\u003c/code> O(trips) unless you also keep a running sum, at which point the list is dead weight.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ambiguous composite keys.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>start + end\u003c/code> as a string key confuses \"AB\" + \"C\" with \"A\" + \"BC\". Use the tuple \u003ccode>(start, end)\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Integer division.\u003c/b> In Python \u003ccode>total / trips\u003c/code> is a float; in Java or C++ cast before dividing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Bucket counts that share a factor with the keys.\u003c/b> With 1000 buckets and keys that are multiples of 1000, every key lands in bucket 0 and each operation becomes O(n). A prime bucket count such as 1009, or a mixing hash, spreads them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Undefined calls.\u003c/b> Decide what \u003ccode>check_out\u003c/code> does for a rider who never checked in; LeetCode promises it never happens, production code must raise a clear error.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run check_in(4, \"X\", 1), check_in(5, \"X\", 2), check_out(4, \"Y\", 6), check_in(4, \"Y\", 7), check_out(5, \"Y\", 12), check_out(4, \"X\", 20). What are \u003ccode>totals\u003c/code>, average(\"X\", \"Y\") and average(\"Y\", \"X\")?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>totals is {(\"X\", \"Y\"): [15, 2], (\"Y\", \"X\"): [13, 1]}. Rider 4 took 5 units from X to Y and rider 5 took 10, so the X-to-Y average is 7.5; rider 4's second trip from Y to X took 13, so that average is 13.0. open_trips ends empty.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your manager now wants the median trip time per pair. Why can the current design not answer it, and what would you store instead?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A sum and a count discard the distribution, so the median is unrecoverable. Keep the trip times per pair: either a list sorted on demand (O(k log k) per query), or two heaps per pair (a max-heap of the lower half and a min-heap of the upper half), which gives O(log k) inserts and an O(1) median.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague's hash set uses 1000 buckets and stores order ids that are all multiples of 1000. Their benchmark shows O(n) behaviour. Why, and what is the smallest fix?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>key % 1000\u003c/code> is 0 for every key, so all n keys share one chain and each operation scans it (in a test with 50 such keys the longest chain was 50). Use a prime number of buckets such as 1009, which spreads multiples of 1000 evenly (longest chain 1 in the same test), or mix the key's bits before taking the remainder.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>705 Design HashSet.\u003c/b> Implement it with buckets and no built-in \u003ccode>set\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>dict\u003c/code>. Expected: O(1 + n/m) per operation; with 1024 buckets, \u003ccode>add(3)\u003c/code> then \u003ccode>add(1027)\u003c/code> puts both in bucket 3 as \u003ccode>[3, 1027]\u003c/code>; after \u003ccode>remove(3)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>contains(3)\u003c/code> is False and \u003ccode>contains(1027)\u003c/code> is still True.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>1396 Design Underground System.\u003c/b> Write the operations table and the invariant first, then the code. Expected: O(1) per call; after check_in(1, \"A\", 3), check_out(1, \"B\", 8), check_in(2, \"A\", 10), check_out(2, \"B\", 22), getAverageTime(\"A\", \"B\") returns 8.5.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to turn a list of operations and costs into fields, an invariant and methods, and how to choose what to store.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:design/how-do-you-design-an-lru-cache-with-o-1-get-and-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An LRU cache needs two things fast: find an entry by key, and know which entry was used least recently. A dict answers the first; a doubly linked list kept in recency order answers the second, because a node can be unlinked in O(1) once the dict hands it to you. Every get or put moves the node to the front; eviction removes the node at the back.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Picture a desk with room for three folders. Whenever you use a folder you put it on top of the pile. When a fourth folder arrives and the desk is full, the folder at the bottom goes back to the cabinet: it is the one you have gone longest without touching. You also keep an index card per folder saying where it is in the pile, so you can pull it out without digging.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The pile is the linked list, the index cards are the dict, and \"pull it out and put it on top\" is unlink plus push to the front.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Why one structure is not enough\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>A \u003cb>dict\u003c/b> alone finds a key in O(1), but has no cheap way to say \"move this key to the most recent end\" and \"remove the oldest\" together with a guarantee.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A \u003cb>list\u003c/b> in recency order knows the oldest (the end), but finding a key and moving it from the middle costs O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>A \u003cb>doubly linked list\u003c/b> can move a node in O(1), but only if you already hold the node. The dict supplies exactly that: \u003ccode>key -&gt; node\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>The layout\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>          most recent                                least recent\nroot &lt;-&gt; [c:3] &lt;-&gt; [a:1] &lt;-&gt; [b:2] &lt;-&gt; root        (circular: root.prev is the LRU node)\n\nmap: {\"a\": node_a, \"b\": node_b, \"c\": node_c}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>One \u003cb>sentinel\u003c/b> node, \u003ccode>root\u003c/code>, sits between the two ends of a circular list. \u003ccode>root.next\u003c/code> is the most recently used node and \u003ccode>root.prev\u003c/code> the least recently used. Because the sentinel is always there, inserting into an empty list, removing the only node, or removing the tail never needs a special case.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The operations\u003c/h5>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>get(key)\u003c/code>: look the node up in the dict; on a miss return -1. On a hit, \u003cb>unlink\u003c/b> it (\u003ccode>node.prev.next = node.next; node.next.prev = node.prev\u003c/code>) and \u003cb>push it to the front\u003c/b> (between root and root.next).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>put(key, value)\u003c/code>: if the key exists, update the value and move the node to the front (a put is a use). Otherwise, if the cache is full, take \u003ccode>root.prev\u003c/code>, unlink it and delete its key from the dict; then create a node, store it in the dict and push it to the front.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>The node stores its own \u003cb>key\u003c/b> for one reason: on eviction you hold the node, and you need its key to delete the dict entry.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The invariant\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ci>The dict and the list contain exactly the same keys, at most \u003ccode>capacity\u003c/code> of them, and the list order from \u003ccode>root.next\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>root.prev\u003c/code> is the order of last use, newest first.\u003c/i> Every method above restores it before returning.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003ccode>get\u003c/code> does one dict lookup (O(1) average) and a constant number of pointer assignments: \u003cb>O(1)\u003c/b>. \u003ccode>put\u003c/code> adds at most one eviction (one unlink, one dict delete) and one insertion: \u003cb>O(1)\u003c/b>. Space is O(capacity): one node per entry (64 bytes for a node with four \u003ccode>__slots__\u003c/code> fields on 64-bit CPython 3.12) plus one dict entry.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Measured on a 200,000-request skewed trace with capacity 1000 (the lab below), a hand-written linked-list version took about 1.5 µs per request, an \u003ccode>OrderedDict\u003c/code> version about 0.5 µs, and \u003ccode>functools.lru_cache\u003c/code> about 0.4 µs. Same complexity; the built-ins win because their linked lists are in C.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use LRU when \u003cb>recency predicts reuse\u003c/b> (temporal locality): user sessions, recently viewed pages, decoded images, database pages. Its weakness is a \u003cb>scan\u003c/b>: one pass over many keys that will never be used again pushes every hot entry out. If a few keys stay popular for a long time, frequency-based eviction (the next card) keeps them better.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>When\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=n)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Memoising a pure function in Python; thread-safe, in C, with \u003ccode>cache_info()\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>OrderedDict\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>move_to_end\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>popitem(last=False)\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You need a cache object with your own API (TTL, delete, size in bytes)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hand-written dict plus linked list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Interviews, other languages, or when you need hooks on every move\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Approximations\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Very large caches where a list per entry costs too much memory: Redis samples 5 keys and evicts the oldest of the sample\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Newer policies such as SIEVE, S3-FIFO and TinyLFU admission are covered in the caching module later in the guide.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Capacity 3. The list is shown most recent first; the dict always holds the same keys.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">put(a, 1), put(b, 2), put(c, 3): three misses, each pushed to the front\u003c/p>\u003cpre>list  root &lt;-&gt; \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> &lt;-&gt; b &lt;-&gt; a &lt;-&gt; root\nmap   a, b, c\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get(a) = 1: hit, so unlink a and push it to the front\u003c/p>\u003cpre>list  root &lt;-&gt; \u003cmark>a\u003c/mark> &lt;-&gt; c &lt;-&gt; b &lt;-&gt; root\nmap   a, b, c\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">put(d, 4): full, so evict root.prev = b (its node tells us the key to delete), then push d\u003c/p>\u003cpre>list  root &lt;-&gt; \u003cmark>d\u003c/mark> &lt;-&gt; a &lt;-&gt; c &lt;-&gt; root\nmap   a, c, d        (\u003cmark>b\u003c/mark> deleted)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get(b) = -1: a miss changes nothing\u003c/p>\u003cpre>list  root &lt;-&gt; d &lt;-&gt; a &lt;-&gt; c &lt;-&gt; root\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">put(c, 30): c exists, so update the value and move it to the front; nothing is evicted\u003c/p>\u003cpre>list  root &lt;-&gt; \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark> &lt;-&gt; d &lt;-&gt; a &lt;-&gt; root\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">put(e, 5): full again; the least recent is now a\u003c/p>\u003cpre>list  root &lt;-&gt; \u003cmark>e\u003c/mark> &lt;-&gt; c &lt;-&gt; d &lt;-&gt; root\nmap   c, d, e        (\u003cmark>a\u003c/mark> deleted)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get(d) = 4: hit, move to front\u003c/p>\u003cpre>list  root &lt;-&gt; \u003cmark>d\u003c/mark> &lt;-&gt; e &lt;-&gt; c &lt;-&gt; root\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class _Node:\n    __slots__ = (\"key\", \"value\", \"prev\", \"next\")\n\n    def __init__(self, key=None, value=None):\n        self.key, self.value = key, value\n        self.prev = self.next = self\n\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    \"\"\"dict key -&gt; node, plus a circular doubly linked list with one sentinel.\n    root.next is the most recently used node, root.prev the least recently used.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.map = {}\n        self.root = _Node()\n\n    def _unlink(self, node):\n        node.prev.next = node.next\n        node.next.prev = node.prev\n\n    def _push_front(self, node):\n        node.prev, node.next = self.root, self.root.next\n        self.root.next.prev = node\n        self.root.next = node\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        node = self.map.get(key)\n        if node is None:\n            return -1\n        self._unlink(node)\n        self._push_front(node)\n        return node.value\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        node = self.map.get(key)\n        if node is not None:\n            node.value = value\n            self._unlink(node)\n            self._push_front(node)\n            return\n        if len(self.map) == self.capacity:\n            lru = self.root.prev\n            self._unlink(lru)\n            del self.map[lru.key]\n        node = _Node(key, value)\n        self.map[key] = node\n        self._push_front(node)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The same behaviour with the standard library, which is what you would ship in Python:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import OrderedDict\n\n\nclass OrderedLRU:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.od = OrderedDict()            # oldest first, newest last\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        if key not in self.od:\n            return -1\n        self.od.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.od[key]\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        if key in self.od:\n            self.od.move_to_end(key)\n        elif len(self.od) == self.capacity:\n            self.od.popitem(last=False)\n        self.od[key] = value\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>A put on an existing key is a use.\u003c/b> Updating the value without moving the node leaves it near the back, so it is evicted too early. Random tests with capacity 2 or more catch this; capacity 1 never does.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Evicting before checking whether the key exists.\u003c/b> A full cache that receives \u003ccode>put\u003c/code> for a key it already holds must not evict anything.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Forgetting the dict on eviction.\u003c/b> Unlinking the tail but leaving \u003ccode>map[key]\u003c/code> behind makes \u003ccode>len(map)\u003c/code> grow past capacity and later \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> calls return evicted values.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Singly linked lists.\u003c/b> Unlinking needs the predecessor; without \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> you would have to search for it in O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Threads.\u003c/b> The pointer updates are several statements; \u003ccode>functools.lru_cache\u003c/code> holds a lock around them, and so must any shared cache of your own.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Capacity 2. Run put(1, 1), put(2, 2), get(1), put(3, 3), get(2), put(1, 10), put(4, 4). What does get(2) return, which keys remain, and in what recency order?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>get(2) returns -1, because put(3, 3) evicted 2 (get(1) had made 1 more recent). put(1, 10) updates 1 and moves it to the front, so put(4, 4) evicts 3. The cache holds 4 then 1 (newest first), with 1 mapped to 10.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why must the list be doubly linked, and why does each node store its key?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Unlinking a node in O(1) needs both neighbours: \u003ccode>prev\u003c/code> to repoint its next pointer and \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> to repoint its prev pointer. A singly linked list would need an O(n) search for the predecessor. The key is stored because eviction starts from the node at \u003ccode>root.prev\u003c/code>, and the only way to delete its dict entry is to know its key.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A teammate's \u003ccode>put\u003c/code> begins with \"if the cache is full, evict root.prev\", then handles the existing-key case. With capacity 2, what does put(\"a\", 1), put(\"b\", 2), put(\"b\", 9), get(\"a\") return, and what should it return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It returns -1: the third call evicts \"a\" even though \"b\" was already present and nothing needed to go. The correct cache returns 1. Check for an existing key first; evict only when inserting a new key into a full cache.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">You need to memoise an expensive pure function of two integers in Python. Do you write your own LRU class?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No. \u003ccode>@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=...)\u003c/code> is implemented in C, is thread-safe, and reports hits and misses through \u003ccode>cache_info()\u003c/code>. Write your own class only when you need behaviour the decorator lacks, such as expiry times, explicit deletes or a size limit in bytes.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>146 LRU Cache.\u003c/b> Implement the dict plus doubly linked list version (no OrderedDict) and then the OrderedDict version. Expected: O(1) per call; with capacity 2, the calls put(5, 50), get(5), put(6, 60), put(5, 55), put(7, 70), get(6), get(5), put(8, 80), get(7), get(8) return 50, -1, 55, -1, 80 for the gets, and the cache ends holding 8 then 5.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to combine a dict with a sentinel-based doubly linked list to get O(1) LRU get and put, and when to use OrderedDict or functools.lru_cache instead.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:design/how-do-you-build-an-lfu-cache-where-every-operat":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Group keys into \u003cb>buckets by use count\u003c/b>, each bucket ordered by recency, and remember the smallest non-empty count in \u003ccode>min_freq\u003c/code>. A use moves a key from bucket f to bucket f + 1, an eviction pops the oldest key of bucket \u003ccode>min_freq\u003c/code>, and a new key always starts at count 1. Because counts only ever change by one, \u003ccode>min_freq\u003c/code> can be maintained without searching.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A corner shop with one small display shelf keeps the snacks that sell most often. When a new product arrives and the shelf is full, the shopkeeper removes the product with the fewest sales so far; if two products are tied, the one that sold least recently goes. To do that instantly, the shopkeeper keeps the products sorted into trays labelled \"sold once\", \"sold twice\", and so on, with each tray in the order of the last sale.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every sale moves a product to the next tray up. The cheapest product to throw out is always the oldest one in the lowest non-empty tray.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>The naive version and its cost\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Store \u003ccode>(count, last_used, key)\u003c/code> in a min-heap and pop the smallest. Each use changes a key's count, which leaves a stale heap entry behind, so you need lazy deletion and every operation costs O(log n). The task asks for O(1).\u003c/p>\u003ch5>The O(1) layout\u003c/h5>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>value:   {x: 1, y: 2, z: 3}          key -&gt; value\ncount:   {x: 3, y: 2, z: 1}          key -&gt; number of uses\nbuckets: 1: [z]   2: [y]   3: [x]    count -&gt; keys, oldest use first (OrderedDict)\nmin_freq = 1                         smallest count that has a non-empty bucket\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ccode>get(key)\u003c/code>: on a hit, \u003cb>touch\u003c/b> the key: remove it from bucket f, append it to bucket f + 1, and if bucket f became empty and f was \u003ccode>min_freq\u003c/code>, set \u003ccode>min_freq = f + 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>put(key, value)\u003c/code> for an existing key: update the value and touch it (a put is a use).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ccode>put\u003c/code> for a new key into a full cache: pop the oldest key of bucket \u003ccode>min_freq\u003c/code> (\u003ccode>popitem(last=False)\u003c/code>), delete it from both dicts, then insert the new key into bucket 1 and set \u003ccode>min_freq = 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch5>Why min_freq never needs a search\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The invariant: \u003ci>bucket f holds exactly the keys used f times, in order of last use, and when the cache is not empty \u003ccode>min_freq\u003c/code> is the smallest f with a non-empty bucket.\u003c/i> Only two events can change the minimum:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>A new key arrives.\u003c/b> It has count 1, and no count is smaller, so \u003ccode>min_freq = 1\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A touch empties the minimum bucket.\u003c/b> The key just moved from f to f + 1, so bucket f + 1 is non-empty and nothing lies between f and f + 1. The new minimum is exactly f + 1.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Eviction can also empty the minimum bucket, but eviction is always followed by inserting a new key, which sets the minimum to 1 anyway.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>All O`one: the same insight with decrements\u003c/h5>\u003cp>LeetCode 432 lets counts go \u003cb>down\u003c/b> as well as up and asks for any key with the maximum or the minimum count. Counts still change by exactly one, so a key moves only to a neighbouring bucket. Keep the buckets themselves in a doubly linked list sorted by count, with head and tail sentinels; \u003ccode>key -&gt; bucket\u003c/code> finds a key's bucket, a missing neighbour bucket is created next to it, and an empty bucket is unlinked. The minimum is \u003ccode>head.next\u003c/code>, the maximum is \u003ccode>tail.prev\u003c/code>, both O(1).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every step is a dict or OrderedDict operation: lookup, delete, append at the end, or \u003ccode>popitem(last=False)\u003c/code>, each O(1) (average for the hashing). \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>put\u003c/code> therefore cost \u003cb>O(1)\u003c/b>. Space is O(capacity) for the keys plus at most one bucket per distinct count in use, which is also O(capacity).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>LFU suits workloads whose popular items \u003cb>stay popular\u003c/b>: a fixed catalogue of product images, DNS names, configuration blobs. It resists one-off scans better than LRU, because a scanned key reaches only count 1 and is the first to go.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Its weaknesses: a key that was popular yesterday keeps its high count forever (\u003cb>cache pollution\u003c/b>), and a brand-new key, at count 1, is always the first victim even if it is about to become popular. Production systems fix both with decay and approximation: Redis's LFU mode keeps a probabilistic Morris counter of a few bits per key, saturating at around a million hits by default, and halves it with time (\u003ccode>lfu-decay-time\u003c/code>, default one minute).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>LRU\u003c/th>\u003cth>Exact LFU (buckets)\u003c/th>\u003cth>Heap-based LFU\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Per operation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(log n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Survives a one-off scan\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Adapts to shifting popularity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Quickly\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Slowly, unless counts decay\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Slowly\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Extra state per key\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two pointers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A count, a bucket slot\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A count, heap entries\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Capacity 2. Buckets are shown as \u003ccode>count: [keys, oldest first]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">put(a, 1), put(b, 2): both new, both in bucket 1; a is older\u003c/p>\u003cpre>buckets  1: [a, \u003cmark>b\u003c/mark>]\nmin_freq \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get(a) = 1: a moves from bucket 1 to bucket 2; bucket 1 still holds b, so min_freq stays 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>buckets  1: [b]   2: [\u003cmark>a\u003c/mark>]\nmin_freq 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">put(c, 3): full, so evict the oldest key in bucket min_freq = 1, which is b; c enters bucket 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>buckets  1: [\u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>]   2: [a]        (\u003cmark>b\u003c/mark> evicted)\nmin_freq 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get(b) = -1: b is gone; nothing changes\u003c/p>\u003cpre>buckets  1: [c]   2: [a]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get(c) = 3: c moves to bucket 2, bucket 1 empties and it was the minimum, so min_freq = 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>buckets  2: [a, \u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>]\nmin_freq \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get(c) = 3 again: c moves to bucket 3; bucket 2 still holds a, so min_freq stays 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>buckets  2: [a]   3: [\u003cmark>c\u003c/mark>]\nmin_freq 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">put(d, 4): full; the least frequent key is a (count 2), so a is evicted; d starts at count 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>buckets  1: [\u003cmark>d\u003c/mark>]   3: [c]        (\u003cmark>a\u003c/mark> evicted)\nmin_freq \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get(a) = -1, then get(d) = 4: d moves to bucket 2 and the minimum follows it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>buckets  2: [\u003cmark>d\u003c/mark>]   3: [c]\nmin_freq \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import OrderedDict, defaultdict\n\n\nclass LFUCache:\n    \"\"\"key -&gt; value, key -&gt; count, count -&gt; keys in recency order (oldest first), and min_freq.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.value = {}\n        self.count = {}\n        self.buckets = defaultdict(OrderedDict)\n        self.min_freq = 0\n\n    def _touch(self, key):\n        f = self.count[key]\n        del self.buckets[f][key]\n        if not self.buckets[f]:\n            del self.buckets[f]\n            if self.min_freq == f:\n                self.min_freq = f + 1\n        self.count[key] = f + 1\n        self.buckets[f + 1][key] = None\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        if key not in self.value:\n            return -1\n        self._touch(key)\n        return self.value[key]\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        if self.capacity &lt;= 0:\n            return\n        if key in self.value:\n            self.value[key] = value\n            self._touch(key)\n            return\n        if len(self.value) == self.capacity:\n            victim, _ = self.buckets[self.min_freq].popitem(last=False)\n            if not self.buckets[self.min_freq]:\n                del self.buckets[self.min_freq]\n            del self.value[victim], self.count[victim]\n        self.value[key] = value\n        self.count[key] = 1\n        self.buckets[1][key] = None\n        self.min_freq = 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>This class was checked against a slow reference that scans every key for the smallest (count, last use) pair, on 3,000 random runs of 200 operations with capacities 0 to 5.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Insert, then evict.\u003c/b> If you add the new key to bucket 1 first and then evict from bucket \u003ccode>min_freq\u003c/code>, you can evict the key you just inserted whenever every other key has a count of 2 or more. Evict first.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Capacity 0.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>put\u003c/code> must do nothing; otherwise the eviction pops from an empty bucket.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Empty buckets left behind.\u003c/b> They are harmless for correctness here but waste memory and confuse debugging; delete them.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ties.\u003c/b> Within a bucket the oldest use goes first. Re-inserting a touched key at the end of the next bucket keeps that order for free.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Capacity 3. Run put(x, 1), put(y, 2), get(x), get(x), put(z, 3), get(y), put(w, 4). Which key is evicted, and what are the buckets and min_freq at the end?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>z is evicted: when w arrives, x has count 3, y count 2 and z count 1, so bucket 1 = [z] is the minimum. The final buckets are 1: [w], 2: [y], 3: [x] with min_freq = 1, and the cache holds w, x and y.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Explain why setting \u003ccode>min_freq = f + 1\u003c/code> inside \u003ccode>_touch\u003c/code> is always right when bucket f becomes empty and f was the minimum.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Before the touch no bucket below f was non-empty, because f was the minimum. The touch moved one key from f to f + 1, so bucket f + 1 now contains at least that key, and bucket f is empty. No count strictly between f and f + 1 exists, so the smallest non-empty bucket is f + 1.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A buggy put inserts the new key into bucket 1, sets min_freq = 1, and only then evicts the oldest key of bucket min_freq if the cache is over capacity. With capacity 2, what does put(a, 1), put(b, 2), get(a), get(b), put(c, 3), get(c) return?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It returns -1. At the third put, a and b both have count 2, so bucket 1 contains only c; the eviction pops c itself. A correct cache evicts a (count 2, older than b), keeps b and c, and get(c) returns 3.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>460 LFU Cache.\u003c/b> Implement the bucket design. Expected: O(1) per call; with capacity 2, put(1, 1), put(2, 2), get(2), get(2), put(3, 3), get(1), get(3), put(4, 4), get(3), get(2), get(4) gives 2, 2, -1, 3, -1, 2, 4 for the gets (put(3, 3) evicts 1; put(4, 4) evicts 3, whose count of 2 is below key 2's count of 3).\u003c/li>\u003cli>**432 All O\u003ccode>one Data Structure.** Use a doubly linked list of count buckets with \u003c/code>key -&gt; bucket`. Expected: O(1) per call; after inc(\"x\"), inc(\"y\"), inc(\"y\"), getMaxKey() is \"y\" and getMinKey() is \"x\"; after dec(\"y\") twice both return \"x\"; after dec(\"x\") both return \"\".\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to make LFU eviction O(1) with frequency buckets and a minimum pointer, and why counts that change by one make the pointer free to maintain.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:design/how-do-you-add-get-min-get-max-or-get-random-to-":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>To answer a new question in O(1), store its answer, and update that stored answer inside every operation that could change it. A min stack keeps \"minimum so far\" next to each entry; a queue built from two such stacks gets its maximum; an array plus a value-to-index map gets O(1) delete and random choice by moving the last element into the hole. The work is in proving each update is O(1) and never forgotten.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A cashier who must announce the smallest note in the till at any moment could count every note each time. Instead, each time a note goes on top of the pile, they pencil on it \"smallest so far: ...\". When the top note leaves, the one beneath already carries the right answer for the smaller pile. The answer travels with the data, so it is never recomputed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Min stack: store the answer with each entry\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Push \u003ccode>(x, min(x, previous minimum))\u003c/code>. The minimum of the stack is the second field of the top entry. A pop removes one entry, and the entry below already knows the minimum of everything beneath it, because the stack below never changed after it was pushed. (LeetCode 155, Min Stack, is in the linear structures module's problem set.)\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Max queue: two stacks\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A queue can be built from two stacks: push onto \u003ccode>inbox\u003c/code>; pop from \u003ccode>outbox\u003c/code>, and when \u003ccode>outbox\u003c/code> is empty move every element of \u003ccode>inbox\u003c/code> onto it (which reverses the order, turning LIFO into FIFO). Make both stacks max-stacks, each entry storing the maximum of its stack so far, and the queue's maximum is the larger of the two tops. A monotonic deque, from the monotonic stacks module, gives the same result for sliding-window maxima.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Random choice: an array plus an index map\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>random.choice\u003c/code> needs a list (O(1) by index); membership and delete need a dict. Keep both: \u003ccode>values\u003c/code> (a list) and \u003ccode>where\u003c/code> (value -&gt; index). Insert appends and records the index. \u003cb>Delete swaps with the last element\u003c/b>: move the last value into the deleted value's slot, update that value's index, then pop the end. Popping the end of a list is O(1); deleting from the middle would shift everything after it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>With duplicates (LeetCode 381), one value can sit at several indices, so \u003ccode>where\u003c/code> maps value -&gt; \u003cb>set\u003c/b> of indices. Delete takes any index of the value, moves the last element there and fixes both index sets.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Running totals\u003c/h5>\u003cp>LeetCode 2276 asks for the number of integers covered by a growing set of intervals. Keep the intervals disjoint and sorted, and keep \u003ccode>total\u003c/code> updated by every \u003ccode>add\u003c/code>: subtract the lengths of the intervals you merge, add the length of the merged one. \u003ccode>count()\u003c/code> then returns a stored number.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Min stack:\u003c/b> push, pop, top and get_min each touch one entry: \u003cb>O(1)\u003c/b> worst case. Space doubles (two numbers per entry).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Max queue:\u003c/b> each element is pushed onto \u003ccode>inbox\u003c/code> once, moved to \u003ccode>outbox\u003c/code> at most once and popped once, so n operations do O(n) work in total: \u003cb>O(1) amortised\u003c/b>, although a single pop can cost O(n). \u003ccode>max\u003c/code> is O(1).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>RandomizedSet:\u003c/b> insert, remove and get_random each do O(1) average dict work and O(1) list work at the end: \u003cb>O(1) average\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Count intervals:\u003c/b> an \u003ccode>add\u003c/code> that merges k intervals costs O(log n + k) with bisect plus the list splice; each interval is created once and merged away at most once, so the merging is amortised, although the list splice is O(n) in the worst case (a balanced tree or \u003ccode>SortedList\u003c/code> makes it O(log n)). \u003ccode>count\u003c/code> is \u003cb>O(1)\u003c/b>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use augmentation when the question (\"current minimum\", \"a random element\", \"total covered\") is asked often and the structure changes by small steps whose effect on the answer you can compute locally.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Need\u003c/th>\u003cth>Augment\u003c/th>\u003cth>Instead of\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Minimum of a stack\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(value, min so far) per entry\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A heap with lazy deletion, O(log n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Maximum of a queue\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two max-stacks, or a monotonic deque\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rescanning, O(n)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Uniform random element with deletes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>List plus index map, delete by swap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A set (no O(1) random choice)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Total covered length\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Running total updated on merge\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Recounting all intervals\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>It fails when one operation can change the answer in a way you cannot compute locally. A min stack works because a pop can only reveal an older minimum that was stored; a plain set with deletions cannot keep its minimum this way, because removing the minimum leaves no stored \"next minimum\" (use a heap or a sorted structure).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>RandomizedSet holding 10, 20, 30, 40. Watch the swap-with-last delete keep the list dense and the map exact.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">start: four values; where maps each value to its index\u003c/p>\u003cpre>values  [10, 20, 30, 40]\nwhere   {10: 0, 20: 1, 30: 2, 40: 3}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">remove(20): its index is 1; pop the last value (40) and write it into slot 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>values  [10, \u003cmark>40\u003c/mark>, 30]\nwhere   {10: 0, 30: 2, \u003cmark>40: 1\u003c/mark>}        (20 deleted)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">remove(40): index 1; pop the last value (30) into slot 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>values  [10, \u003cmark>30\u003c/mark>]\nwhere   {10: 0, \u003cmark>30: 1\u003c/mark>}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">insert(50): append at index 2 and record it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>values  [10, 30, \u003cmark>50\u003c/mark>]\nwhere   {10: 0, 30: 1, \u003cmark>50: 2\u003c/mark>}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">remove(50): it is already the last element, so popping it is the whole job\u003c/p>\u003cpre>values  [10, 30]\nwhere   {10: 0, 30: 1}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get_random(): random.choice(values) returns 10 or 30, each with probability 1/2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>values  [\u003cmark>10\u003c/mark>, \u003cmark>30\u003c/mark>]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\n\nclass MinStack:\n    \"\"\"Each entry stores (value, minimum of the stack up to and including it).\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        m = x if not self.items else min(x, self.items[-1][1])\n        self.items.append((x, m))\n\n    def pop(self):\n        return self.items.pop()[0]\n\n    def top(self):\n        return self.items[-1][0]\n\n    def get_min(self):\n        return self.items[-1][1]\n\n\nclass MaxQueue:\n    \"\"\"A FIFO queue from two max-stacks: push onto inbox, pop from outbox, refill when empty.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.inbox, self.outbox = [], []        # entries are (value, max so far in that stack)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _push(stack, x):\n        stack.append((x, x if not stack else max(x, stack[-1][1])))\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self._push(self.inbox, x)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        if not self.outbox:\n            while self.inbox:\n                self._push(self.outbox, self.inbox.pop()[0])\n        return self.outbox.pop()[0]\n\n    def max(self):\n        return max(s[-1][1] for s in (self.inbox, self.outbox) if s)\n\n\nclass RandomizedSet:\n    \"\"\"values: a list for O(1) random choice; where: value -&gt; its index in the list.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.values = []\n        self.where = {}\n\n    def insert(self, x):\n        if x in self.where:\n            return False\n        self.where[x] = len(self.values)\n        self.values.append(x)\n        return True\n\n    def remove(self, x):\n        i = self.where.pop(x, None)\n        if i is None:\n            return False\n        last = self.values.pop()\n        if i &lt; len(self.values):          # x was not the last element: move last into its slot\n            self.values[i] = last\n            self.where[last] = i\n        return True\n\n    def get_random(self):\n        return random.choice(self.values)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>All three were stress-tested against plain Python models (a list for the stack and queue, a set for RandomizedSet) on thousands of random operation sequences; 80,000 calls to \u003ccode>get_random\u003c/code> on eight values gave each value a frequency within one percentage point of 1/8.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deleting from the middle of the list.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>values.remove(x)\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>del values[i]\u003c/code> is O(n); always swap with the last element.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The last-element case.\u003c/b> If x is the last element, there is nothing to move. The template pops first and only writes back when \u003ccode>i &lt; len(values)\u003c/code>. Code that deletes \u003ccode>where[x]\u003c/code> \u003ci>before\u003c/i> writing \u003ccode>where[last] = i\u003c/code> re-creates a stale entry for x when x is the last element.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Random choice from a set or dict.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>random.choice\u003c/code> needs a sequence; converting \u003ccode>list(s)\u003c/code> each time is O(n).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Max queue amortisation.\u003c/b> The O(1) is amortised: one pop may move the whole inbox. If a single slow call matters, the monotonic deque has O(1) worst-case pops (its pushes are the amortised part instead).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Push 4, 2, 6, 1 onto the MinStack. What does \u003ccode>items\u003c/code> contain, and what is get_min() after two pops?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>items is [(4, 4), (2, 2), (6, 2), (1, 1)]. Two pops remove (1, 1) and (6, 2), leaving [(4, 4), (2, 2)], so get_min() is 2, read directly from the top entry.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Prove that the two-stack queue does O(n) total work for any sequence of n operations, although one pop can take O(n).\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Follow one element: it is pushed onto inbox once, moved from inbox to outbox at most once (it never goes back), and popped from outbox at most once. That is at most three constant-time steps per element, so n operations cost at most about 3n steps in total. A single pop that triggers a refill pays for every element moved, but each of those elements is moved only once in its life.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">This remove deletes the map entry first: \u003ccode>i = where.pop(x); last = values[-1]; values[i] = last; where[last] = i; values.pop()\u003c/code>. On which input does it break, and how?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When x is the last element. Then last is x, so \u003ccode>where[last] = i\u003c/code> writes x back into the map, pointing at index i, and the pop removes that index. Afterwards insert(x) returns False (x looks present) and a later remove(x) reads a stale index. Handle the last-element case first, as the template does.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>380 Insert Delete GetRandom O(1).\u003c/b> Expected: O(1) average per call; insert(4), insert(4), insert(9), remove(4), remove(4) return True, False, True, True, False, and getRandom() then always returns 9.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>381 Insert Delete GetRandom O(1), duplicates allowed.\u003c/b> Map each value to a set of indices. Expected: O(1) average per call; insert(2), insert(2), insert(5) return True, False, True; after remove(2) returns True, the list holds one 2 and one 5, and getRandom() returns each with probability 1/2.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2276 Count Integers in Intervals.\u003c/b> Keep sorted disjoint intervals and a running total. Expected: add(2, 4), add(8, 9) gives count() = 5; add(5, 7) merges everything into [2, 9] and count() = 8; add(1, 3) gives count() = 9.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to answer extra queries in O(1) by storing their answers and updating them inside every operation, including delete-by-swap for O(1) random choice.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:design/how-do-you-store-values-that-change-over-time-an":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Never overwrite history you may be asked about. For each key keep a list of (time, value) pairs in time order; a query for time t is a binary search for the last entry at or before t. Snapshots become cheap the same way: record only the cells that changed, tagged with the snapshot id. When values can be corrected, keep one dict as the source of truth and treat every other index (heaps, sorted lists) as a cache you validate against it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A shared document's version history does not store a full copy of the document for every save. It stores what changed and when. To see the document as it was on Tuesday at noon, you take the latest change to each part made on or before that moment. The question \"what was the value at time t?\" is a search in a sorted list of changes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Time-based key-value store\u003c/h5>\u003cp>\u003ccode>set(key, value, t)\u003c/code> appends \u003ccode>(t, value)\u003c/code> to the key's history; LeetCode 981 (in the binary search module's problem set) promises that timestamps arrive in increasing order, so appending keeps each history sorted. \u003ccode>get(key, t)\u003c/code> returns the value of the \u003cb>last entry with time &lt;= t\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>bisect_right(times, t) - 1\u003c/code>, or the empty string if that index is -1. Keeping times and values in two parallel lists lets bisect compare plain numbers.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Snapshot array\u003c/h5>\u003cp>An array of n cells supports \u003ccode>set(i, v)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>snap()\u003c/code> (returns a snapshot id 0, 1, 2, ...) and \u003ccode>get(i, snap_id)\u003c/code>. Copying the array on every snap costs O(n) per snap. Instead, give each cell its own history of \u003ccode>(snap_id, value)\u003c/code>, starting with \u003ccode>(-1, 0)\u003c/code>, and append only when a cell changes. Several sets to the same cell before the next snap overwrite the last entry, since only the final value of that snapshot is visible. \u003ccode>get(i, s)\u003c/code> is a bisect for the last entry with snap id &lt;= s. LeetCode 1146, Snapshot Array, appears in one of the mixed review sets.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Corrections: one truth, lazy indexes\u003c/h5>\u003cp>LeetCode 2034 streams (timestamp, price) records that may \u003cb>correct\u003c/b> an earlier timestamp, and asks for the latest price, the maximum and the minimum. Keep \u003ccode>price: timestamp -&gt; price\u003c/code> as the source of truth and the latest timestamp as a number. For the maximum, push \u003ccode>(-price, timestamp)\u003c/code> onto a max-heap on every update and never delete from it. When asked for the maximum, look at the top: if \u003ccode>price[timestamp]\u003c/code> no longer equals that price, the entry is stale (that timestamp was corrected later), so pop it and look again. The first entry that agrees with the dict is the true maximum.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This \u003cb>lazy validation\u003c/b> pattern appears whenever an index is cheaper to rebuild piecemeal than to update in place: heaps with changed priorities, caches with expiry, search indexes with deleted documents.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>TimeMap:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>set\u003c/code> is an O(1) amortised append; \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> is a bisect, \u003cb>O(log h)\u003c/b> where h is that key's history length. Space O(total sets).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>SnapshotArray:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>set\u003c/code> O(1) amortised, \u003ccode>snap\u003c/code> O(1), \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> \u003cb>O(log c)\u003c/b> where c is the number of changes to that cell. Space O(n + total sets), against O(n x snaps) for copying.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>StockPrice:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>update\u003c/code> is two heap pushes, O(log u) for u updates so far. A \u003ccode>maximum\u003c/code> call may pop several stale entries, but each pushed entry is popped at most once, so over the whole stream the pops cost O(u log u) in total: \u003cb>O(log u) amortised\u003c/b> per operation. Space O(u), because stale entries wait in the heaps until they surface.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Cues: \"at time t\", \"as of\", \"snapshot\", \"version\", \"undo\", \"history\", or updates that may arrive for an earlier key. Production versions are everywhere: PostgreSQL's MVCC keeps several versions of each row so that every statement reads a consistent snapshot, time-series databases search sorted timestamps, and Git stores history as a chain of snapshots that share unchanged content.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Situation\u003c/th>\u003cth>Choose\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Timestamps arrive in order per key\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Append plus bisect\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Writes may arrive out of order\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>bisect.insort\u003c/code> (O(h) per insert) or a \u003ccode>SortedList\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Whole-array snapshots, few changes per snap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Per-cell histories\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Corrections to past records, max or min wanted\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dict of truth plus heaps with lazy validation\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Avoid lazy heaps when corrections vastly outnumber queries and memory is tight: stale entries pile up. Rebuild the heap from the dict when it grows past, say, twice the number of live records.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>SnapshotArray of length 3. Each cell's history is a list of (snap id, value), starting at (-1, 0).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">set(0, 5): cell 0 changes during snapshot 0, so append (0, 5)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cell 0  (-1,0) \u003cmark>(0,5)\u003c/mark>\ncell 1  (-1,0)\ncell 2  (-1,0)\nsnap_id 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">snap() returns 0: nothing is copied, only the counter moves\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cell 0  (-1,0) (0,5)\nsnap_id \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">set(0, 6), set(2, 1): new entries tagged with snap id 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cell 0  (-1,0) (0,5) \u003cmark>(1,6)\u003c/mark>\ncell 2  (-1,0) \u003cmark>(1,1)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">set(2, 4): cell 2 already changed during snap 1, so overwrite that entry instead of appending\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cell 2  (-1,0) \u003cmark>(1,4)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">snap() returns 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>snap_id \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get(0, 0): last entry of cell 0 with id &lt;= 0 is (0, 5), so 5; get(0, 1) gives 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cell 0  (-1,0) \u003cmark>(0,5)\u003c/mark> (1,6)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">get(2, 0): cell 2's only entry with id &lt;= 0 is (-1, 0), so 0; get(1, 1) is 0 too\u003c/p>\u003cpre>cell 2  \u003cmark>(-1,0)\u003c/mark> (1,4)\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\nfrom bisect import bisect_right\n\n\nclass TimeMap:\n    \"\"\"key -&gt; parallel lists of increasing timestamps and their values.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.times = {}\n        self.vals = {}\n\n    def set(self, key, value, t):\n        self.times.setdefault(key, []).append(t)     # assumes t never decreases per key\n        self.vals.setdefault(key, []).append(value)\n\n    def get(self, key, t):\n        ts = self.times.get(key)\n        if not ts:\n            return \"\"\n        i = bisect_right(ts, t) - 1                   # last write at or before t\n        return self.vals[key][i] if i &gt;= 0 else \"\"\n\n\nclass SnapshotArray:\n    \"\"\"Each index keeps a history of (snap_id, value); untouched indices cost nothing.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, length):\n        self.hist = [[(-1, 0)] for _ in range(length)]\n        self.snap_id = 0\n\n    def set(self, index, val):\n        h = self.hist[index]\n        if h[-1][0] == self.snap_id:\n            h[-1] = (self.snap_id, val)        # overwrite within the same snapshot\n        else:\n            h.append((self.snap_id, val))\n\n    def snap(self):\n        self.snap_id += 1\n        return self.snap_id - 1\n\n    def get(self, index, snap_id):\n        h = self.hist[index]\n        i = bisect_right(h, (snap_id, float(\"inf\"))) - 1\n        return h[i][1]\n\n\nclass StockPrice:\n    \"\"\"The dict is the truth; the heaps are indexes that may hold stale (price, time) pairs.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.price = {}\n        self.latest = 0\n        self.maxh, self.minh = [], []\n\n    def update(self, t, p):\n        self.price[t] = p\n        self.latest = max(self.latest, t)\n        heapq.heappush(self.maxh, (-p, t))\n        heapq.heappush(self.minh, (p, t))\n\n    def current(self):\n        return self.price[self.latest]\n\n    def maximum(self):\n        while self.price[self.maxh[0][1]] != -self.maxh[0][0]:\n            heapq.heappop(self.maxh)          # stale: that timestamp was corrected later\n        return -self.maxh[0][0]\n\n    def minimum(self):\n        while self.price[self.minh[0][1]] != self.minh[0][0]:\n            heapq.heappop(self.minh)\n        return self.minh[0][0]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each class was compared with a brute force (scan the full log, keep full array copies, recompute max and min from the dict) on 2,000 random operation sequences.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>bisect_left instead of bisect_right.\u003c/b> For \"last entry at or before t\" you need the insertion point \u003ci>after\u003c/i> any entry equal to t; \u003ccode>bisect_left\u003c/code> would skip an entry written exactly at t.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Index -1.\u003c/b> If every entry is after t, \u003ccode>bisect_right(...) - 1\u003c/code> is -1, and \u003ccode>vals[-1]\u003c/code> silently returns the newest value. Check for it, or seed each history with a sentinel as SnapshotArray does.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Comparing tuples with mixed types.\u003c/b> Bisecting \u003ccode>(snap_id, value)\u003c/code> pairs with \u003ccode>(s, float(\"inf\"))\u003c/code> works for numbers; for other values, bisect a separate list of ids.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Validating against the wrong truth.\u003c/b> A stale heap entry is detected by comparing its price with \u003ccode>price[t]\u003c/code>, not by remembering which entries were pushed; two updates with the same price at the same timestamp are both valid.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">SnapshotArray(2): set(1, 4), snap(), snap(), set(1, 9), set(0, 2), snap(). What do the three snaps return, what are the histories, and what are get(1, 0), get(1, 1), get(1, 2) and get(0, 1)?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The snaps return 0, 1 and 2. Cell 0's history is [(-1, 0), (2, 2)] and cell 1's is [(-1, 0), (0, 4), (2, 9)]. get(1, 0) and get(1, 1) are 4 (snapshot 1 recorded no change to cell 1, so it inherits snapshot 0's value), get(1, 2) is 9, and get(0, 1) is 0.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why is StockPrice's \u003ccode>maximum\u003c/code> O(log u) amortised even though one call may pop many entries?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every update pushes exactly one entry per heap, and an entry, once popped as stale, never returns. So across the whole stream the total number of pops is at most the number of pushes, u, each O(log u). Spreading that total over u operations gives O(log u) amortised per operation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A colleague's TimeMap uses \u003ccode>bisect_left(times, t) - 1\u003c/code>. With writes at times 1, 5 and 9, which query returns the wrong answer?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any query exactly at a write time: get(key, 5) finds index 1 with bisect_left, subtracts one, and returns the value written at 1 instead of the value written at 5. bisect_right puts the insertion point after equal times, so subtracting one lands on the entry written at t.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>2034 Stock Price Fluctuation.\u003c/b> Keep the dict of truth plus a max-heap and a min-heap with lazy validation. Expected: O(log n) amortised per call; after update(4, 30), update(7, 12), update(4, 5), update(9, 20), update(7, 40), current() is 20, maximum() is 40 and minimum() is 5 (the entries (30, 4) and (12, 7) are stale and get popped when they surface).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exercise: TimeMap.\u003c/b> Implement it and run set(\"door\", \"shut\", 1), set(\"door\", \"open\", 5), set(\"door\", \"shut\", 9). Expected: get(\"door\", t) for t = 0, 1, 4, 5, 8, 9, 100 returns \"\", \"shut\", \"shut\", \"open\", \"open\", \"shut\", \"shut\", and get(\"lamp\", 3) returns \"\".\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to answer \"value at time t\" with per-key histories and bisect, take snapshots by recording only changes, and keep heaps correct under corrections with lazy validation.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:design/how-do-you-count-recent-events-and-limit-request":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\"How many events in the last W seconds?\" is a queue of timestamps: append the new one, pop old ones from the front. A rate limiter is that question plus a decision. Keeping every timestamp (a \u003cb>sliding log\u003c/b>) is exact but costs memory per request; a \u003cb>fixed window\u003c/b> counter is one integer but can let through twice the limit across a boundary; a \u003cb>sliding window counter\u003c/b> blends two windows; a \u003cb>token bucket\u003c/b> stores two numbers and allows controlled bursts.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A nightclub allows at most 100 people inside per hour of arrivals. The doorman could write down every arrival time and count the last hour (exact, but a long list). He could reset a tally on the hour (cheap, but 100 people at 9:59 and 100 more at 10:00 all get in). Or he could hand out tickets from a jar that a machine refills at a steady rate, letting a group in at once if the jar is full: that is the token bucket.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Hit counter: a queue of timestamps\u003c/h5>\u003cp>LeetCode 933 calls \u003ccode>ping(t)\u003c/code> with increasing t and asks how many pings fall in [t - 3000, t]. Append t to a deque; while the front is older than t - 3000, pop it; return the length. Old timestamps never matter again, so discarding them is safe.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Sliding log limiter\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Allow a request at time t if fewer than L requests were allowed in the window (t - W, t]. Keep the allowed timestamps in a deque, drop those &lt;= t - W, then compare the length with L. It is \u003cb>exact\u003c/b>: no window of length W ever contains more than L allowed requests. Memory is up to L timestamps per client.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Because the log never needs more than L entries, it fits in a \u003cb>ring buffer of size L\u003c/b> (LeetCode 622 builds one): keep the last L allowed times with the oldest at \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>. If that oldest time is still inside the window, all L are, so reject; otherwise overwrite it with t and advance \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>. Every call is O(1) worst case.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Fixed window counter\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Number the windows \u003ccode>w = t // W\u003c/code> and keep (current window, count). A new window resets the count. One integer per client, but a burst of L requests at the end of one window and L more at the start of the next passes 2L requests within a few seconds. In a measured boundary test (L = 10, W = 60, ten requests at t = 59 and ten at t = 60) the fixed window allowed all 20, the sliding log 10.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Sliding window counter\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Keep the counts of the current and previous fixed windows and estimate the sliding count as \u003ccode>prev x (1 - f) + cur\u003c/code>, where f is how far t is into the current window. It assumes the previous window's requests were spread evenly. Cloudflare has described running this two-counter approximation at scale. In the same boundary test it allowed 10.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Token bucket\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The bucket holds up to \u003ccode>burst\u003c/code> tokens and gains \u003ccode>rate\u003c/code> tokens per second. On each request, first refill lazily: \u003ccode>tokens = min(burst, tokens + (now - last) x rate)\u003c/code>, then spend one token if at least one is there. No timer runs in the background; the refill is computed from the elapsed time. Over any interval of length T, at most \u003ccode>burst + rate x T\u003c/code> requests pass, so the long-run rate is \u003ccode>rate\u003c/code> and the largest burst is \u003ccode>burst\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Limiter\u003c/th>\u003cth>Time per request\u003c/th>\u003cth>Memory per client\u003c/th>\u003cth>Guarantee\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sliding log (deque)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) amortised: each timestamp is appended once and popped once\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up to L timestamps\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exact: at most L in every window\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sliding log (ring of size L)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1) worst case\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exactly L slots\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Exact, same decisions as the deque\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Fixed window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two integers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>At most L per aligned window, up to 2L across a boundary\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sliding window counter\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Three numbers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Approximate; exact only if traffic is even\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Token bucket\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(1)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Two floats\u003c/td>\u003ctd>At most burst + rate x T in any interval T\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The hit counter is the same as the deque log: O(1) amortised per ping, O(hits in the window) memory.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exactness matters and L is small\u003c/b> (login attempts, password resets): sliding log.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Huge numbers of clients, memory is the constraint\u003c/b>: fixed window or sliding counter; accept the boundary effect, or use the sliding counter to blunt it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>APIs that should allow short bursts but cap the average\u003c/b>: token bucket. Its state per client is two numbers, so it can live in a shared store.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Smoothing traffic rather than rejecting it\u003c/b>: a leaky bucket queues requests and releases them at a fixed rate; NGINX's \u003ccode>limit_req\u003c/code> works this way.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Production concerns (distributed counters, clock skew, per-client state eviction) belong to the production caching and limiting material later in the guide; the data structure choices are the ones above.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Token bucket with rate 1 token per second and burst 3, starting full at t = 0.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">t = 0: bucket full at 3.0; first request takes a token\u003c/p>\u003cpre>tokens before 3.0   -&gt;  \u003cmark>allow\u003c/mark>   -&gt;  tokens 2.0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">t = 0, twice more: no time has passed, so no refill\u003c/p>\u003cpre>tokens 2.0 -&gt; allow -&gt; 1.0;  tokens 1.0 -&gt; allow -&gt; \u003cmark>0.0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">t = 0, fourth request: empty bucket\u003c/p>\u003cpre>tokens before 0.0   -&gt;  \u003cmark>reject\u003c/mark>  -&gt;  tokens 0.0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">t = 1.5: refill 1.5 x 1 = 1.5 tokens, then spend one\u003c/p>\u003cpre>tokens before \u003cmark>1.5\u003c/mark>   -&gt;  allow   -&gt;  tokens 0.5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">t = 2: refill 0.5 x 1 = 0.5, reaching 1.0; allow; a second request at t = 2 finds 0.0 and is rejected\u003c/p>\u003cpre>tokens before \u003cmark>1.0\u003c/mark>   -&gt;  allow, then \u003cmark>reject\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">t = 5: three seconds would add 3.0, but the bucket caps at burst = 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>tokens before \u003cmark>3.0\u003c/mark> (capped)   -&gt;  allow, allow, allow, then reject\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Eleven requests, eight allowed: the three-request burst at t = 0, one each at 1.5 and 2, and three at 5. The bound burst + rate x T = 3 + 1 x 5 = 8 is met exactly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\n\nclass RecentCounter:\n    \"\"\"Hits in [t - window, t], inclusive. Times must not decrease.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, window=3000):\n        self.window = window\n        self.q = deque()\n\n    def ping(self, t):\n        self.q.append(t)\n        while self.q[0] &lt; t - self.window:\n            self.q.popleft()\n        return len(self.q)\n\n\nclass RingLimiter:\n    \"\"\"At most `limit` requests in any window (t - window, t]: remember the last `limit` allowed times.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, limit, window):\n        self.window = window\n        self.times = [float(\"-inf\")] * limit   # ring buffer of allowed times, oldest at head\n        self.head = 0\n\n    def allow(self, t):\n        if self.times[self.head] &gt; t - self.window:\n            return False                       # the last `limit` allowed requests are all inside the window\n        self.times[self.head] = t\n        self.head = (self.head + 1) % len(self.times)\n        return True\n\n\nclass TokenBucket:\n    \"\"\"Refill `rate` tokens per second up to `burst`; a request spends one token.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, rate, burst, now=0.0):\n        self.rate, self.burst = rate, burst\n        self.tokens, self.last = float(burst), now\n\n    def allow(self, now, cost=1):\n        self.tokens = min(self.burst, self.tokens + (now - self.last) * self.rate)\n        self.last = now\n        if self.tokens &gt;= cost:\n            self.tokens -= cost\n            return True\n        return False\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>These were property-tested on random traffic: the ring limiter made the same decision as the deque log on 3,000 random runs; no window of length W ever held more than L requests allowed by the log; and over every interval the token bucket allowed at most burst + rate x T.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inclusive or exclusive edges.\u003c/b> LeetCode 933 counts [t - 3000, t], so pop while \u003ccode>front &lt; t - 3000\u003c/code>. The limiter above uses (t - W, t], so it drops times \u003ccode>&lt;= t - W\u003c/code>. Write the interval down before writing the comparison.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fixed windows at boundaries.\u003c/b> They can admit 2L requests in a short span. Say so when you choose one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Timers for refills.\u003c/b> Never run a background job per client to add tokens; compute the refill from the elapsed time on each request.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Clocks that go backwards.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>time.time()\u003c/code> can jump; use \u003ccode>time.monotonic()\u003c/code> for elapsed time in a single process.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Unbounded per-client state.\u003c/b> A dict of limiters keyed by client id grows forever; evict idle clients (an LRU cache of limiters works well).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A token bucket has rate 2 per second and burst 4, full at t = 0. Requests arrive at t = 0 (five of them), t = 0.5 (two) and t = 3 (one). Which are allowed, and how many tokens are left at the end?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At t = 0 the first four are allowed and the fifth rejected (bucket empty). At t = 0.5 the refill adds 1 token: the first request is allowed, the second rejected. At t = 3 the refill would add 5 but the bucket caps at 4, so the request is allowed and 3 tokens remain.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the ring-buffer limiter reject exactly when the deque sliding log would?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The ring holds the last L allowed timestamps with the oldest at \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>. The log allows t only if fewer than L allowed times lie in (t - W, t]. Since times never decrease, the L most recent allowed times are all inside that window exactly when the oldest of them is; so \"the oldest is &gt; t - W\" is the same condition as \"L allowed times are already in the window\".\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A limiter of 5 requests per 10 seconds receives five requests at t = 9 and five at t = 10. How many does a fixed window allow, and how many does the sliding window counter allow?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The fixed window allows all 10: t = 9 falls in window 0 and t = 10 in window 1, each with its own count of 5. The sliding counter allows 5: at t = 10 it is 0% into window 1, so the estimate is 5 x (1 - 0) + 0 = 5, which already reaches the limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>933 Number of Recent Calls.\u003c/b> Use a deque of timestamps. Expected: O(1) amortised per ping; pings at 5, 1000, 3005, 3006, 7000 return 1, 2, 3, 3, 1.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>622 Design Circular Queue.\u003c/b> Store \u003ccode>head\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>size\u003c/code>, and derive the tail as \u003ccode>(head + size - 1) % k\u003c/code>. Expected: O(1) per call; with k = 2, enQueue(7), enQueue(8), enQueue(9) return True, True, False, Rear() is 8, isFull() is True, deQueue() is True, enQueue(9) is True, and then Front() is 8 and Rear() is 9 (the 9 was written into the slot the 7 vacated).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to count events in a sliding window with a queue and how to choose between sliding log, fixed window, sliding counter and token bucket limiters by their guarantees and memory.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:design/how-do-you-design-iterators-that-flatten-peek-or":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>An iterator is a cursor plus a promise: it remembers where it is and produces one element per request, so memory stays proportional to the cursor, not to the data. The design rule is to \u003cb>buffer one element of lookahead\u003c/b>: \u003ccode>has_next\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>peek\u003c/code> fill the buffer if it is empty and never consume more, \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> hands the buffer over and clears it. For nested data, the cursor is a stack of positions, one per open level.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Reading a long book, you keep a bookmark, not a photocopy of the remaining pages. To answer \"is there another chapter?\" you glance ahead without turning the page; doing that twice must not skip a chapter. For a book of nested sections, you keep one bookmark per open section: when a subsection ends, you remove its bookmark and continue in the parent.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>Python's protocol\u003c/h5>\u003cp>An iterable has \u003ccode>__iter__\u003c/code>, which returns an iterator. An iterator has \u003ccode>__next__\u003c/code>, which returns the next element or raises \u003ccode>StopIteration\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>for\u003c/code> loops, \u003ccode>list()\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sum()\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>zip()\u003c/code> all speak this protocol. The built-in \u003ccode>next(it, default)\u003c/code> returns \u003ccode>default\u003c/code> instead of raising, which is the cleanest way to read one element of lookahead.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Peeking iterator: one element of lookahead\u003c/h5>\u003cp>LeetCode 284 wraps an iterator and adds \u003ccode>peek()\u003c/code>. Store the next element in a field as soon as you can: in \u003ccode>__init__\u003c/code> read one element ahead; \u003ccode>peek\u003c/code> returns the field; \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> returns it and reads the following element into the field; \u003ccode>has_next\u003c/code> checks whether the field holds a real element. Use a private sentinel object for \"exhausted\", never \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>, because \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> may be a legitimate element.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Flattening nested lists: a stack of cursors\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Given \u003ccode>[1, [2, [3]], 4]\u003c/code>, produce 1, 2, 3, 4 one at a time. Keep a stack of \u003ccode>[list, index of the next unread item]\u003c/code>. To find the next integer: look at the top entry; if its list is finished, pop it and continue with the parent; if the next item is a list, advance the index and push a new cursor for the sublist (without copying it); if it is an integer, advance the index, store the integer, and stop. LeetCode 341 (Flatten Nested List Iterator) is this exact design and sits in the linear structures module's problem set.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Making has_next idempotent\u003c/h5>\u003cp>LeetCode drives iterators with \u003ccode>while it.hasNext(): it.next()\u003c/code>, but real callers may call \u003ccode>has_next\u003c/code> twice. Keep a \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> flag: \u003ccode>has_next\u003c/code> does work only when \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> is False and sets it when it finds an element; \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> calls \u003ccode>has_next\u003c/code>, clears \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> and returns the buffered value.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>Generators do the bookkeeping for you\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A function containing \u003ccode>yield\u003c/code> returns a generator: Python suspends the function at each \u003ccode>yield\u003c/code>, keeping its local variables and position, and resumes it on the next request. A recursive generator with \u003ccode>yield from\u003c/code> flattens nested lists in four lines, and the chain of suspended frames \u003ci>is\u003c/i> the stack of cursors. Interviewers usually ask for the explicit class; in your own code, prefer the generator.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Peeking iterator:\u003c/b> every method is \u003cb>O(1)\u003c/b> plus at most one call to the wrapped iterator. Space O(1).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Nested iterator:\u003c/b> each integer is buffered once and each list is pushed and popped once, so producing all n integers from a structure with m lists costs O(n + m) in total, \u003cb>O(1) amortised per element\u003c/b>. Space is O(d), where d is the nesting depth, against O(n) for flattening everything into a list up front.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recursive generator:\u003c/b> the same total work, but each element is passed up through every suspended generator above it, so an element at depth d costs O(d) to deliver, and Python's recursion limit (about 1000 frames) caps the depth. The explicit stack has neither problem.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use lazy iterators when the data is large, unbounded or expensive to produce: lines of a file (looping over a file object reads one line at a time, which the Python tutorial calls memory efficient and fast), rows from a database cursor, pages from a paginated API, or an infinite sequence such as \u003ccode>itertools.count()\u003c/code>. They also let a consumer stop early without paying for the rest.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Approach\u003c/th>\u003cth>Memory\u003c/th>\u003cth>First element available\u003c/th>\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Flatten to a list, then iterate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(n)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>After all n are built\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simple; fine for small inputs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Explicit stack iterator class\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(depth)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Immediately\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Interview standard; no recursion limit\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Recursive generator with \u003ccode>yield from\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>O(depth) frames\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Immediately\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Shortest; limited by recursion depth\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not make an iterator lazy if the caller needs \u003ccode>len()\u003c/code>, random access or several passes; an iterator can be consumed only once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Flattening \u003ccode>[1, [2, [3]], 4]\u003c/code> with a stack of \u003ccode>[list, next index]\u003c/code> cursors. \u003ccode>L@i\u003c/code> means \"list L, next unread index i\".\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">start: one cursor on the outer list\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [1, [2, [3]], 4]@\u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">has_next(): item 0 is the integer 1; advance the index and buffer it\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [1, [2, [3]], 4]@\u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nvalue  \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>  ready\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next() returns 1. has_next(): item 1 is a list, so advance to 2 and push a cursor for [2, [3]]; its item 0 is 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [1, [2, [3]], 4]@2   \u003cmark>[2, [3]]@1\u003c/mark>\nvalue  \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next() returns 2. has_next(): [2, [3]] item 1 is the list [3], push it; its item 0 is 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [1, [2, [3]], 4]@2   [2, [3]]@2   \u003cmark>[3]@1\u003c/mark>\nvalue  \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next() returns 3. has_next(): [3] is finished, pop; [2, [3]] is finished, pop; outer item 2 is 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  [1, [2, [3]], 4]@\u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\nvalue  \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">next() returns 4. has_next(): the outer list is finished, pop it; the stack is empty, so False\u003c/p>\u003cpre>stack  \u003cmark>(empty)\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class PeekingIterator:\n    \"\"\"Wraps any iterator; keeps one element of lookahead in self._next.\"\"\"\n    _END = object()\n\n    def __init__(self, it):\n        self._it = iter(it)\n        self._next = next(self._it, self._END)\n\n    def peek(self):\n        return self._next          # caller should check has_next() first\n\n    def next(self):\n        x = self._next\n        self._next = next(self._it, self._END)\n        return x\n\n    def has_next(self):\n        return self._next is not self._END\n\n\nclass NestedIterator:\n    \"\"\"Flattens nested lists lazily. Stack entries are [list, index of its next unread item].\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, nested):\n        self.stack = [[nested, 0]]\n        self.ready = False                   # True when self.value holds the next integer\n        self.value = None\n\n    def has_next(self):\n        while not self.ready and self.stack:\n            lst, i = self.stack[-1]\n            if i == len(lst):\n                self.stack.pop()             # this list is finished: resume its parent\n                continue\n            self.stack[-1][1] += 1\n            if isinstance(lst[i], list):\n                self.stack.append([lst[i], 0])   # descend without copying the sublist\n            else:\n                self.value, self.ready = lst[i], True\n        return self.ready\n\n    def next(self):\n        if not self.has_next():\n            raise StopIteration\n        self.ready = False\n        return self.value\n\n\ndef flatten(nested):\n    \"\"\"Generator version: Python keeps the stack of suspended frames for you.\"\"\"\n    for x in nested:\n        if isinstance(x, list):\n            yield from flatten(x)\n        else:\n            yield x\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>All three were checked against a recursive list-building flatten on 5,000 random nested lists (with \u003ccode>has_next\u003c/code> called an extra time at random), and the peeking iterator was also run over an infinite generator.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>has_next that consumes.\u003c/b> If \u003ccode>has_next\u003c/code> pulls from the underlying iterator without buffering, a second call skips an element.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>None as \"no element\".\u003c/b> \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> (or 0, or \"\") may be a real element; use a private sentinel object.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Copying sublists.\u003c/b> Pushing \u003ccode>lst[i][:]\u003c/code> or reversing a copy costs O(size) per list; push a cursor instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Empty sublists.\u003c/b> \u003ccode>[[], [[]]]\u003c/code> contains no integers; \u003ccode>has_next\u003c/code> must keep popping until it finds one or the stack empties, which the \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> loop does.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exhausted generators.\u003c/b> A generator can be iterated once; \u003ccode>list(gen)\u003c/code> a second time returns \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For \u003ccode>NestedIterator([[], [[5]], 6])\u003c/code>, what do has_next(), has_next(), next(), next(), has_next() return in that order?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>True, True, 5, 6, False. The first has_next pops past the empty list, descends into [[5]] and [5], and buffers 5; the second call sees \u003ccode>ready\u003c/code> already True and does nothing. The first next returns 5; the second next calls has_next, which finishes [5] and [[5]] and buffers 6; the final has_next empties the stack and returns False.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why does the explicit-stack iterator use O(depth) memory while flattening into a list uses O(n), and why can the recursive generator fail where the class does not?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The stack holds one small cursor per currently open list, at most the nesting depth, while a flattened list holds every element. The recursive generator also keeps one suspended frame per open level, but those are Python call frames, so a list nested deeper than the recursion limit (about 1000 by default) raises RecursionError; the explicit stack is an ordinary list with no such limit.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A PeekingIterator stores \u003ccode>None\u003c/code> to mean \"exhausted\". Which input breaks it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any iterator that yields \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>, for example \u003ccode>[1, None, 3]\u003c/code>: after 1 is returned, the buffer holds \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>, so \u003ccode>has_next\u003c/code> reports False and the caller never sees None or 3. A private sentinel such as \u003ccode>object()\u003c/code> cannot collide with real data.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>284 Peeking Iterator.\u003c/b> Buffer one element. Expected: O(1) per call; over [7, 8, 9], next(), peek(), next(), next(), hasNext() return 7, 8, 8, 9, False.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exercise: generator flatten.\u003c/b> Write \u003ccode>flatten\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>yield from\u003c/code>, then the explicit-stack version as a generator. Expected: both give [1, 1, 2, 1, 3, 4] for \u003ccode>[[1, 1], 2, [1, [], [3, [4]]]]\u003c/code> and [] for \u003ccode>[[[]], []]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to build lazy iterators with one element of lookahead, flatten nested data with a stack of cursors, and when a generator is the simpler choice.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:design/how-do-you-test-a-data-structure-you-designed":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A designed structure is only as good as its invariant after the ten-thousandth operation, and hand-written examples rarely reach the states where bugs live. Write a \u003cb>model\u003c/b>: the same interface implemented in the most obviously correct way, ignoring speed (a list, a scan, a \u003ccode>min()\u003c/code>). Drive both with the same random operations, compare every result and invariant, and when they disagree, \u003cb>shrink\u003c/b> the failing sequence to the shortest one that still fails.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Intuition\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A bank replacing its interest calculator does not trust a dozen spot checks. It runs the new system and the old one side by side on the same transactions for a month and investigates every difference. The old system is slow and ugly, but trusted; that is what makes it a good judge. In testing, that trusted twin is the \u003cb>model\u003c/b> (also called the oracle or reference implementation).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>How it works\u003c/h4>\u003ch5>1. Write the model\u003c/h5>\u003cp>The model has the same methods and the simplest correct implementation: an LRU cache as a list in recency order with linear scans; an LFU cache as a dict scanned for the smallest (count, last use); a min stack as a list and \u003ccode>min()\u003c/code>. If the model needs cleverness, it is not a model.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>2. Generate operations from a small space\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Bugs live where operations interact: the same key updated twice, an eviction right after an update, a pop that empties the structure. Use \u003cb>small parameters\u003c/b> so those collisions happen constantly: keys 0 to 6, capacities 1 to 3, values from a short range. A key space of a million makes repeated keys rare.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>3. Compare after every operation\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Compare each return value, and check the structure's own invariants (size never above capacity, dict and list hold the same keys) after each step. Stop at the first disagreement and print the sequence that led to it.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>4. Shrink\u003c/h5>\u003cp>A random failure may be fifty operations long. Shrinking removes operations and simplifies arguments while the failure persists, until nothing more can be removed. You can do this by hand (delete one line at a time and rerun), but \u003cb>Hypothesis\u003c/b> does it automatically: a \u003ccode>RuleBasedStateMachine\u003c/code> declares rules (operations with argument strategies), preconditions (when a rule may run) and invariants (checked after every step), and on failure prints a minimal program that reproduces the bug.\u003c/p>\u003ch5>5. Keep the test\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Run it in CI with a fixed budget. A planted bug is the test of the test: break the structure on purpose (forget to refresh recency on update) and make sure the test fails.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Cost\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each trial runs s operations on both objects. If the model costs O(n) per operation on a structure of size n, a test of T trials costs O(T x s x n), which is trivial for the small sizes that find bugs: 2,000 trials of 12 steps finish in well under a second. Hypothesis adds shrinking time only when something fails. The real cost is writing the model, usually 15 to 30 lines, and it pays for itself the first time it catches an ordering bug.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When to use it\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use model-based testing for anything with state and more than one operation: caches, limiters, iterators, heaps, union-find, interval sets, your LeetCode design classes before you submit them. It is the stateful version of the stress test you use for single functions.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Technique\u003c/th>\u003cth>Finds\u003c/th>\u003cth>Misses\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hand-written examples\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The cases you thought of\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Interactions you did not imagine\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Random stress test against a model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Wrong results in reachable states\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bugs needing very large sizes; the model's own bugs\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hypothesis state machine\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The same, plus a minimal reproducing program\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The same blind spots, if the strategies are too narrow\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Differential test against another implementation (e.g. \u003ccode>OrderedDict\u003c/code>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Disagreements, with no model to write\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bugs shared by both implementations\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For randomised operations (get_random) compare properties, not values: the result is a member of the model's set, and over many calls each member appears about equally often.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked trace\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A buggy min stack remembers one minimum and never restores it after a pop. Hypothesis found a failure and shrank it to three operations; here is that run, system under test beside the model.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">push(1): both agree; invariant get_min() == min(model) holds\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sut    items [1]      min \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nmodel  [1]            min(model) \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">push(0): the new value is smaller, so both minimums become 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sut    items [1, 0]   min \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\nmodel  [1, 0]         min(model) \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">pop(): both return 0, but the sut never restores its minimum\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sut    items [1]      min \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\nmodel  [1]            min(model) \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">the invariant fails, and Hypothesis prints the minimal program:\u003c/p>\u003cpre>state = MinStackMachine()\nstate.push(x=1)\nstate.push(x=0)\nstate.pop()\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A dependency-free harness, usable for any class with a matching model:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\n\ndef stress(make_sut, make_model, random_op, trials=2000, max_steps=12, seed=0):\n    \"\"\"Run random operation sequences on both objects; return the first failing log, or None.\"\"\"\n    rng = random.Random(seed)\n    for _ in range(trials):\n        sut, model, log = make_sut(), make_model(), []\n        for _ in range(rng.randint(1, max_steps)):\n            name, args = random_op(rng, model)\n            want = getattr(model, name)(*args)\n            got = getattr(sut, name)(*args)\n            log.append(f\"{name}({', '.join(map(repr, args))}) -&gt; {got!r}\")\n            if got != want:\n                return log, f\"expected {want!r}\"\n    return None\n\n\nclass ModelMinStack:\n    \"\"\"Obviously correct and slow: a list, and min() over it.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.items = []\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.items.append(x)\n\n    def pop(self):\n        return self.items.pop()\n\n    def get_min(self):\n        return min(self.items)\n\n\ndef random_op(rng, model):\n    if model.items and rng.random() &lt; 0.4:\n        return rng.choice([\"pop\", \"get_min\"]), ()\n    return \"push\", (rng.randint(-3, 3),)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The Hypothesis version (\u003ccode>pip install hypothesis\u003c/code>; run it with pytest or unittest):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from hypothesis import strategies as st\nfrom hypothesis.stateful import RuleBasedStateMachine, invariant, precondition, rule\n\n\nclass MinStackMachine(RuleBasedStateMachine):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.sut = MinStack()        # system under test\n        self.model = []              # obviously correct, slow reference\n\n    @rule(x=st.integers(-10, 10))\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.sut.push(x)\n        self.model.append(x)\n\n    @precondition(lambda self: self.model)\n    @rule()\n    def pop(self):\n        assert self.sut.pop() == self.model.pop()\n\n    @precondition(lambda self: self.model)\n    @invariant()\n    def same_min(self):\n        assert self.sut.get_min() == min(self.model)\n\n\nTestMinStack = MinStackMachine.TestCase\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>With the correct \u003ccode>MinStack\u003c/code> from the augmentation card this passed 300 examples of up to 40 steps; with the buggy one it failed every time and printed a three-step program, either the one in the trace or, in another run, push(-1), pop(), push(0). Shrinking finds \u003ci>a\u003c/i> minimal failure, not always the same one. The plain harness, run on the buggy stack with seed 0, returned the six-step log push(3), push(-2), pop(), push(2), pop(), get_min() -&gt; -2, expected 3.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>A clever model.\u003c/b> If the model shares code or tricks with the structure, it shares its bugs. Make it slow and obvious.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>A key space that is too large.\u003c/b> With keys up to 10^9, two operations almost never touch the same key, and update and eviction bugs never show. Use 5 to 10 keys.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Only capacity 1.\u003c/b> Many recency and frequency bugs need at least two entries to compete: the planted \"update does not refresh recency\" LRU bug passed every run at capacity 1 and failed at capacities 2 and 3.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Comparing random results by value.\u003c/b> For get_random, check membership and frequencies, not equality with the model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Real time inside the structure.\u003c/b> Limiters and expiring caches must take the clock as an argument or an injected function, so tests can control time instead of sleeping.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your LRU stress test uses capacity 1 and keys from 0 to 1,000,000, and it has never failed. Why is that weak evidence, and what two changes would you make?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>With capacity 1 every put evicts the only other entry, so recency order never matters and ordering bugs cannot show; with a million keys, repeated keys (updates and hits on existing entries) almost never happen. Use capacities 1 to 3 and keys 0 to 6 so that updates, hits and evictions interleave constantly.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Hypothesis reports a failing LFU run of six steps: put(1, 0), put(0, 0), get(0), get(1), put(2, 0), get(0). The model returns -1 for the last get, your cache returns 0. Capacity is 2. What is the bug?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At put(2, 0) both keys have count 2 and key 0 was used less recently, so the model evicts 0. Your cache kept 0, so it must have evicted 2 itself: it inserted the new key into bucket 1 before evicting from the minimum bucket, which by then was bucket 1 containing only the new key. Evict first, then insert.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why should the test compare return values and also check invariants after every operation?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Some bugs corrupt internal state without changing any return value yet, for example a dict entry left behind on eviction. An invariant check (dict size equals list length, size at most capacity) catches the corruption at the step where it happens, so the shrunk example ends at the faulty operation instead of many steps later when a wrong value finally surfaces.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Lab\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>**432 All O\u003ccode>one Data Structure.** Before submitting, test your class against a model built on \u003c/code>collections.Counter` (max and min by scanning the counter), with five keys and random inc and dec calls (dec only on present keys). Expected: 3,000 random runs of 60 operations pass, checking that the returned max and min keys have the model's maximum and minimum counts (several keys can tie, so compare counts, not keys).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>460 LFU Cache.\u003c/b> Write a Hypothesis state machine with get and put rules over keys 0 to 4 and capacity 2, against a model that scans for the smallest (count, last use). Expected: the correct cache passes 500 examples; with the insert-then-evict bug planted, Hypothesis prints a six-step failing program like the one in the second question above.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to test any stateful structure against a slow model with random operations, and how Hypothesis shrinks a failure to a minimal program.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:design/lru-cache-tested-and-measured":"\u003cp>Real caches are judged by two numbers: how often they hit and how long each request takes. This lab makes you produce both, but only after a test has convinced you the cache is correct.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>In \u003ccode>lru.py\u003c/code>, write \u003ccode>LRUCache\u003c/code> (dict plus circular doubly linked list with a sentinel) and \u003ccode>OrderedLRU\u003c/code> (OrderedDict) from the LRU card, with \u003ccode>get(key)\u003c/code> returning -1 on a miss and \u003ccode>put(key, value)\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write \u003ccode>ModelLRU\u003c/code>: a list of [key, value] pairs, most recent first, where get and put scan the list. It is O(capacity) per call and obviously correct.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In \u003ccode>test_lru.py\u003c/code>, write a \u003ccode>RuleBasedStateMachine\u003c/code> with a \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> rule and a \u003ccode>put\u003c/code> rule over keys 0 to 6 and values 0 to 99, and an invariant that the cache never holds more than \u003ccode>capacity\u003c/code> keys and holds as many keys as the model. Build one machine per class and capacity (1, 2 and 3) and run them with \u003ccode>max_examples=500\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Plant a bug: subclass \u003ccode>LRUCache\u003c/code> so that \u003ccode>put\u003c/code> on an existing key updates the value but does not move the node to the front. Run the machines again and read the shrunk failing program.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Generate a skewed trace: 200,000 requests over 10,000 keys drawn from a Zipf distribution with exponent s = 1.0 (weights 1/rank^s; use \u003ccode>itertools.accumulate\u003c/code> on the weights and \u003ccode>bisect\u003c/code> on a uniform random number, with \u003ccode>random.Random(42)\u003c/code>, and shuffle the key names so popular keys are not simply the small numbers).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Replay the trace through each cache (on a miss, put the key) for capacities 10, 100, 1000 and 5000, and record the hit rate. Repeat for s = 0.8 and s = 1.2.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Time 200,000 requests at capacity 1000 for \u003ccode>LRUCache\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>OrderedLRU\u003c/code> and a function wrapped in \u003ccode>functools.lru_cache(maxsize=1000)\u003c/code>, taking the best of three runs with \u003ccode>time.perf_counter\u003c/code>, and report nanoseconds per request.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write three sentences: which implementation you would ship in Python, how hit rate depends on capacity and skew, and what the planted bug taught you about test parameters.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Both correct caches pass every machine. The planted bug \u003cb>passes at capacity 1\u003c/b> (with one slot, recency never matters) and \u003cb>fails at capacities 2 and 3\u003c/b>, with a shrunk program of five or six steps such as put(0), put(2), put(0), put(1), get(0): the cache evicted 0 although it had just been updated.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Hit rates (measured for this lab; your exact numbers will differ slightly with the generator):\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Capacity\u003c/th>\u003cth>s = 0.8\u003c/th>\u003cth>s = 1.0\u003c/th>\u003cth>s = 1.2\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>10\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.03\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.13\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.34\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>100\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.16\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.39\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.66\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>1000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.43\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.67\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.86\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>5000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.78\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.89\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.95\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Both of your implementations give identical hit rates, because they make identical eviction decisions. Hit rate rises with capacity with diminishing returns, and a more skewed trace (larger s) hits far more often at small capacities. - Latency: on the machine used to write this lab, about 1.5 µs per request for the hand-written linked list, 0.5 µs for \u003ccode>OrderedLRU\u003c/code> and 0.4 µs for \u003ccode>functools.lru_cache\u003c/code>. Expect the same order on yours: identical O(1) complexity, but the C implementations are three to four times faster than pure Python pointer updates. Ship \u003ccode>OrderedDict\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>functools.lru_cache\u003c/code> in Python; write the linked list when the language or the interview requires it.\u003c/p>","p:705":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Implement an integer set with add, remove and membership operations without using a built-in hash set or map. Adding a present key and removing an absent key leave the collection unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keys are integers from 0 through 1,000,000 inclusive. This finite, reasonably small universe permits direct addressing: the key itself identifies a slot. The number of operations can be large, so scanning stored keys on every query is undesirable. We trade fixed memory for worst-case constant operation time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You must build a set of integers without any built-in hash table, and keys go up to a million.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Split the keys into many small groups by a cheap function of the key, so each operation looks at one small group only.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Operations first, then compose structures\u003c/p>\u003cp>An array of m buckets indexed by key % m, each a short list (chaining). With n keys spread evenly a bucket holds about n/m keys, so add, remove and contains are O(1 + n/m) expected. Because keys are bounded by 10^6, a bytearray of 10^6 + 1 flags is an O(1) worst-case alternative using 1 MB.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1 + n/m) expected per operation, O(m + n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep a list of present keys. Scan it to answer membership and to locate a deletion; also scan before inserting to avoid duplicates. Each operation can take O(n) for n stored keys, using O(n) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store one byte per possible key. Its value is one exactly when that key belongs to the set. Assigning a byte twice is harmless, as is clearing an already clear byte. The invariant holds initially because all bytes are zero, and each mutation changes exactly the slot named by its key.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start empty; add 7 twice\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slot 7 becomes 1 and remains 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Remove 7, then ask for 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slot 7 becomes 0; answer \u003cmark>False\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Remove 7 again\u003c/p>\u003cpre>slot 7 stays 0; other slots do not change\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The bounded universe is essential to this implementation; arbitrary integers would need a different representation or input validation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class MyHashSet:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.present = bytearray(1_000_001)\n\n    def add(self, key):\n        self.present[key] = 1\n\n    def remove(self, key):\n        self.present[key] = 0\n\n    def contains(self, key):\n        return bool(self.present[key])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Initialization takes O(U) time and space for U = 1,000,001 possible keys. Every later operation takes O(1) worst-case time and O(1) extra space. The allocation depends on the universe, not the number actually stored.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Allocate one extra slot for the inclusive maximum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keys outside the contract are not checked; negative Python indices would otherwise address the wrong slot.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A bytearray is direct addressing, not a dynamically sized hash table.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Pack membership bits to reduce the constant factor in space.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use chained buckets if the key universe becomes too large for direct addressing.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:933":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Process a sequence of calls, each with a timestamp in milliseconds. For each new call return the number of calls in the inclusive interval from 3000 milliseconds before that timestamp through the current timestamp.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Timestamps are positive and strictly increasing. The current call is itself in the answer. A call exactly 3000 milliseconds old still counts. Increasing time is what makes discarded calls permanently irrelevant; this implementation does not support out-of-order events.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Each call brings a later timestamp and asks how many calls fell in the last 3000 ms, inclusive.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Once a timestamp is too old for the current call, can it ever matter again?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sliding windows and token buckets\u003c/p>\u003cp>Times only increase, so a timestamp that falls out of [t - 3000, t] is out forever. Append t to a deque and pop from the left while the front is below t - 3000; the deque length is the answer. Each timestamp is appended once and popped once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) amortised per call, O(calls in the window) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep every past timestamp. For each new timestamp scan the list and count those within the interval. After n calls the worst-case total is O(n²), and all n timestamps remain stored.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The active timestamps form a suffix of arrival order. Append the current time, then remove old timestamps from the front while they are strictly below the lower boundary. Every remaining time is at least the boundary and no greater than the current time. Every discarded time is even older, and cannot reenter any future window because future boundaries only move forward.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Calls at 1 and 1000\u003c/p>\u003cpre>both are in the window; latest answer \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Call at 3001\u003c/p>\u003cpre>boundary is 1, so the first call remains; answer \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Call at 3002\u003c/p>\u003cpre>boundary is 2; remove 1 and answer \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Each timestamp enters once and leaves at most once, even if a single call removes many old entries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import deque\n\nclass RecentCounter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.times = deque()\n\n    def ping(self, t):\n        self.times.append(t)\n        while self.times[0] &lt; t - 3000:\n            self.times.popleft()\n        return len(self.times)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each call costs O(1) amortized, though one call may remove O(n) entries. Space is O(w), the number of calls currently in the window. It can be O(n) if all calls remain active.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use \u003ccode>&lt;\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>&lt;=\u003c/code>, when expiring the inclusive lower endpoint.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Append the new call before counting it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A queue of arrival order is insufficient for out-of-order timestamps.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Change the window length while keeping it fixed across the stream.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return a weighted sum by maintaining a running total as entries expire.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:622":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build a bounded FIFO queue that supports insertion at the rear, removal at the front, inspection of both ends, and empty/full queries. Insertions and removals return whether they succeeded; an empty end inspection returns -1.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Capacity k is positive and fixed. Values are nonnegative, so -1 is an unambiguous empty result. The number of operations can greatly exceed k, so a queue that never reuses vacated positions would eventually fail despite having room. Duplicate values are allowed and have no special treatment.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A queue with a fixed capacity, no resizing, and O(1) enqueue and dequeue at opposite ends.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Store elements in a fixed array and let the positions wrap around the end. What is the least state you need to know where the front and the back are?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Sliding windows and token buckets\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep head and size. The front is buf[head], the rear is buf[(head + size - 1) % k], enqueue writes at (head + size) % k, dequeue advances head = (head + 1) % k. Keeping size rather than a tail index removes the full-or-empty ambiguity when head equals tail.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) per operation, O(k) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use a regular list in queue order. Append for insertion and delete index zero for removal, rejecting an insertion when the list has k items. Deleting the first item shifts the remaining entries and takes O(k) time; the list uses O(k) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Treat the array as a ring. Keep \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, the physical index of the first element, and \u003ccode>size\u003c/code>, the number of live elements. Logical offset j corresponds to \u003ccode>(head + j) % k\u003c/code>. The next free rear slot is at offset size; the last live element is at offset size - 1. Size distinguishes a completely full ring from an empty one even when their head positions coincide.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Initially size is zero. A successful enqueue fills exactly the next free slot and increases size. A successful dequeue advances head and decreases size, leaving all other logical offsets in the same FIFO order. Full and empty operations do not mutate the invariant.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For example, after a dequeue the former second element becomes logical offset zero. The head can wrap repeatedly without moving any element. We deliberately retain the old bytes in an unused slot: they have no meaning until an enqueue overwrites that slot, and end inspections first check the size.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Capacity 2; enqueue 8, enqueue 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>head 0, size 2, physical slots [8, 4]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Dequeue and enqueue 6\u003c/p>\u003cpre>head 1; new element wraps to slot 0\nlogical queue [4, 6]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Inspect both ends\u003c/p>\u003cpre>front \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>, rear \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>; queue is full\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class MyCircularQueue:\n    def __init__(self, k):\n        self.buf = [0] * k\n        self.head = 0\n        self.size = 0\n\n    def enQueue(self, value):\n        if self.isFull():\n            return False\n        self.buf[(self.head + self.size) % len(self.buf)] = value\n        self.size += 1\n        return True\n\n    def deQueue(self):\n        if self.isEmpty():\n            return False\n        self.head = (self.head + 1) % len(self.buf)\n        self.size -= 1\n        return True\n\n    def Front(self):\n        return -1 if self.isEmpty() else self.buf[self.head]\n\n    def Rear(self):\n        return -1 if self.isEmpty() else self.buf[(self.head + self.size - 1) % len(self.buf)]\n\n    def isEmpty(self):\n        return self.size == 0\n\n    def isFull(self):\n        return self.size == len(self.buf)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every method takes O(1) time. The fixed buffer occupies O(k) space; each call uses O(1) extra space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Check fullness before writing, and emptiness before reading or advancing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Capacity one still works: both ends point to the same slot.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Old values in vacated cells are irrelevant because size determines membership.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A zero-capacity queue is outside the stated contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Implement a bounded deque with insertion and removal at both ends.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Use a resizing ring buffer when capacity need not remain fixed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1396":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain passenger check-ins and check-outs for a transit network, then answer the average completed journey duration for an ordered pair of stations. A journey from A to B is distinct from one from B to A.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each rider has at most one open journey, and a check-out refers to a matching earlier check-in. Times are integers and checkout follows check-in. Queries refer to a station pair with at least one completed trip. Station names and rider identifiers can be used as dictionary keys. These validity guarantees let methods avoid defining error responses for malformed events.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Check-in and check-out events, and queries for an average trip time between two named stations.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>List what each query needs to know. Does the average need every individual trip?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Operations first, then compose structures\u003c/p>\u003cp>Two dicts: rider -&gt; (start station, time) for open trips, and (start, end) -&gt; [total time, count] for finished ones. Check-out pops the open trip and adds its duration to the pair; the average is total / count. Store aggregates, not the trips.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) per operation, O(riders inside + station pairs) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store each completed trip with its start, end and duration. For each average query, scan all completed trips, summing and counting matching pairs. Check-in and check-out remain constant expected time, but a query takes O(t) for t completed trips and storage grows with all t records.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>An average depends only on the sum and count, not the individual durations. Keep open trips by rider, and for each ordered station pair maintain the sum and count of completed durations. At check-out, remove the rider's open entry and update exactly one pair. This avoids counting a trip before it finishes and prevents an old check-in from being reused.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Inductively, each open entry represents one active rider, and each aggregate incorporates exactly the completed trips for its pair. The quotient is therefore the desired arithmetic mean. Separate entries for reversed pairs are necessary because direction affects the answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The aggregate is updated once per completed journey, regardless of how many riders travel simultaneously. Python's division returns a floating-point result, so clients comparing numerical answers should allow the problem's specified tolerance rather than rounding each individual duration.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Rider 5 enters Oak at time 3 and exits Pine at 11\u003c/p>\u003cpre>(Oak, Pine) has total 8, count 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Rider 9 travels Oak to Pine from 4 to 18\u003c/p>\u003cpre>total becomes 22, count 2\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Query Oak to Pine\u003c/p>\u003cpre>average \u003cmark>11.0\u003c/mark>; the reverse direction is separate\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class UndergroundSystem:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.open = {}\n        self.trips = {}\n\n    def checkIn(self, rider, station, t):\n        self.open[rider] = (station, t)\n\n    def checkOut(self, rider, station, t):\n        start, start_time = self.open.pop(rider)\n        pair = (start, station)\n        total, count = self.trips.get(pair, (0, 0))\n        self.trips[pair] = (total + t - start_time, count + 1)\n\n    def getAverageTime(self, start, end):\n        total, count = self.trips[(start, end)]\n        return total / count\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each operation is O(1) expected time, assuming dictionary operations and bounded station-name lengths. Space is O(a + p) for a active riders and p distinct completed station pairs; trip history is not retained. If names have unbounded length, hashing and comparing them also costs time proportional to their length.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Do not average averages: accumulate a sum and its matching count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Remove the active entry at checkout so a rider may start another journey.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Do not mix opposite directions or include unfinished trips.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Invalid check-outs and unknown query pairs are outside the contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Maintain variance using the sum of squared durations as well.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Retain individual trips if later cancellation or time-range queries are required.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:146":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Design a bounded key-value cache. A successful lookup and every insertion or update make the key most recently used. On insertion into a full cache, remove the least recently used key. Return -1 for a missing lookup.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keys and values are nonnegative integers, so -1 indicates a miss. The stated capacity is positive; the implementation also handles zero capacity. A put of an existing key replaces its value without evicting anything and counts as a use. Both operations need constant expected time rather than a scan through the capacity.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A fixed-capacity key-value store whose get and put must both be O(1), evicting the entry unused for the longest time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>One structure finds keys; another keeps recency order and can move or remove any entry in O(1) if you can reach it directly. How does the first give you direct access into the second?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Hash map plus doubly linked list (LRU)\u003c/p>\u003cp>A dict from key to node of a doubly linked list kept in recency order around a sentinel. get and put move the node to the front; eviction removes root.prev and deletes its key, which is why nodes store their keys. A put on an existing key is a use and must move the node too.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) per operation, O(capacity) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store values and a logical timestamp of their latest use. On a full insertion scan all keys to find the smallest timestamp and delete that key. Lookups and updates are expected O(1), but eviction takes O(c) for capacity c; storage is O(c).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Combine a hash map with an ordered doubly linked list. Python's \u003ccode>OrderedDict\u003c/code> provides this composition: key lookup, moving a known key to the end and popping the first key are expected O(1). Maintain least recent at the beginning and most recent at the end. On a hit move the key to the end; on a put, insert or replace and then move to the end. If the size exceeds capacity, pop the first item.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The invariant holds initially for the empty cache. A hit or update removes just that key from its old position and places it last, preserving the relative order of every other key. A new key becomes newest, and the first key is exactly the eviction candidate if capacity is exceeded. A miss changes nothing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Capacity 2; put (1, 10), put (2, 20)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>least to most recent: [1, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Get 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>10\u003c/mark>; order becomes [2, 1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Put (3, 30)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>evict 2; order becomes [1, 3], and get 2 gives \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.items = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        if key not in self.items:\n            return -1\n        self.items.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.items[key]\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        self.items[key] = value\n        self.items.move_to_end(key)\n        if len(self.items) &gt; self.capacity:\n            self.items.popitem(last=False)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each get or put takes O(1) expected time with hash-table operations. Space is O(c), with at most c entries retained between calls; an insertion can temporarily add one extra entry. Worst-case adversarial hash collisions are not constant time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>An update is a use, even if the value stays the same.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A missing lookup must not change recency or create a key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Evict the oldest end, not the newest end.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Capacity zero immediately evicts every insertion.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Implement the list explicitly with sentinels and a key-to-node map.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add expiration times, recognizing that expiry and recency are separate orderings.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:380":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Support insertion, deletion and uniformly random selection from a set of integers. Insertion and deletion return whether the set changed; random selection is called only when the set is nonempty.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Values can be negative, zero or positive. Duplicates are not stored. Each random result must have the same probability among currently present values, not among historical insertions. The required expected constant-time operations rule out shifting an arbitrary suffix on deletion.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A set that must also return a uniformly random element, with insert, remove and random all O(1) on average.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Random choice in O(1) needs positions 0..n-1 with no gaps. How can you delete from the middle of an array without shifting everything?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Augment a structure with synchronised extra state\u003c/p>\u003cp>A list of values plus a dict from value to index. To remove x, move the last value into x's slot, update that value's index, and pop the end; if x is the last element, just pop. random.choice on the list is then uniform.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) average per operation, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store the values in a list. A random position is easy to sample, but membership checks and locating a deletion can scan O(n) entries; deleting from the middle also shifts O(n) entries. Alternatively a set supports membership but constructing a list for each random query takes O(n).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use a dense array of values and a map from each value to its array index. To erase an interior entry, copy the last array value into its position, fix that value's index, and pop the last slot. No order needs to be preserved. Sampling a uniformly random array index then selects each value once, so all currently stored values have equal probability.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Initially both structures are empty. Insertion appends a previously absent value and records its position. Deletion updates the one displaced value and removes the deleted mapping, so the index map remains the inverse of the array. This also handles deleting the last entry: the moved value is the one being removed and its mapping is deleted afterwards.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The array order is deliberately unstable. A client must not infer insertion order from it; this freedom is exactly what avoids shifting elements. Uniformity depends on the random generator choosing indices uniformly, not on the values themselves being consecutive or similarly distributed.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 4, 7, 9\u003c/p>\u003cpre>array [4, 7, 9]; indices {4: 0, 7: 1, 9: 2}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Remove 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>move 9 into slot 1, then pop\narray [4, 9]; indices {4: 0, 9: 1}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Choose either slot uniformly\u003c/p>\u003cpre>each of 4 and 9 has probability \u003cmark>1/2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\nclass RandomizedSet:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.values = []\n        self.index = {}\n\n    def insert(self, val):\n        if val in self.index:\n            return False\n        self.index[val] = len(self.values)\n        self.values.append(val)\n        return True\n\n    def remove(self, val):\n        if val not in self.index:\n            return False\n        i = self.index[val]\n        last = self.values[-1]\n        self.values[i] = last\n        self.index[last] = i\n        self.values.pop()\n        del self.index[val]\n        return True\n\n    def getRandom(self):\n        return random.choice(self.values)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>All methods take O(1) expected time, including amortized dynamic-array append and expected dictionary operations. Space is O(n) for n current values. Random selection relies on \u003ccode>random.choice\u003c/code> selecting array positions uniformly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Update the moved value's index even when deleting an interior slot.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Delete the requested mapping after updating the last value, including when both values are the same.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reject duplicate insertions and missing removals without changing state.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Calling random selection on an empty set is outside the contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Permit duplicates by mapping each value to a set of positions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sample several distinct values without replacement, specifying how state should change.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:284":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Wrap an iterator with a \u003ccode>peek\u003c/code> operation that returns the next value without consuming it. Also provide \u003ccode>next\u003c/code> to consume that value and \u003ccode>hasNext\u003c/code> to report whether one exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The underlying input is a finite Python iterator. It may yield any value, including None, so an ordinary value cannot safely signal exhaustion. Calls to peek and next are valid only when an element exists; this wrapper raises \u003ccode>StopIteration\u003c/code> otherwise. Repeated peeks and availability checks must not advance the logical sequence.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Wrap an existing iterator and add a method that shows the next element without consuming it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The underlying iterator cannot un-read an element. What could you hold on to?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Lazy iterators and generators\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep one element of lookahead in a field, read in the constructor. peek returns it, next returns it and reads the following one, hasNext checks whether the field holds a real element (use a private sentinel for exhausted, not None).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) per call, O(1) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Read the entire iterator into a list and maintain a cursor. Peek and next then take O(1) time, but construction consumes O(n) time and extra space, and the approach cannot support a long or unbounded stream lazily.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain one buffered value and a distinct private sentinel for exhaustion. Fill the buffer once during construction. Peek reads the buffer without altering it; next returns the buffer and refills it from the underlying iterator. \u003ccode>hasNext\u003c/code> compares by identity with the sentinel. Since the underlying iterator has already advanced past the buffer, the logical remaining sequence is the buffer followed by its remaining elements.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Initially the invariant follows from one attempted read. Peek and hasNext leave it unchanged. Next removes exactly its first logical item and refills with the next underlying item; an exhausted iterator instead leaves the sentinel. The sentinel is a fresh object inaccessible to callers, so a yielded None is not confused with exhaustion.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The wrapper performs one-element read-ahead, which is observable if the source iterator has side effects. It does not duplicate or rewind the underlying source. Consumers should advance only through the wrapper once it is constructed, since an external advance could otherwise skip a value.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Wrap iterator [None, 5]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>buffer is None; hasNext is \u003cmark>True\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Peek twice\u003c/p>\u003cpre>both return None; logical sequence is unchanged\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Next twice\u003c/p>\u003cpre>returns None then 5; buffer becomes exhausted\nhasNext is \u003cmark>False\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class PeekingIterator:\n    def __init__(self, iterator):\n        self.iterator = iter(iterator)\n        self.end = object()\n        self.buffer = next(self.iterator, self.end)\n\n    def peek(self):\n        if not self.hasNext():\n            raise StopIteration\n        return self.buffer\n\n    def next(self):\n        value = self.peek()\n        self.buffer = next(self.iterator, self.end)\n        return value\n\n    def hasNext(self):\n        return self.buffer is not self.end\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The wrapper uses O(1) additional space. Construction and next each perform at most one underlying advance; peek and hasNext are O(1). The stated time assumes each underlying advance is O(1); a costly or blocking iterator adds its own cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>None and other falsey values can be real elements.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Repeated peek or hasNext must not consume the next element.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Empty input is exhausted immediately; peek and next raise \u003ccode>StopIteration\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A source with externally shared consumption can invalidate the wrapper's assumptions.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Support Python's \u003ccode>__iter__\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>__next__\u003c/code> protocol on the wrapper.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Buffer k values to support looking farther ahead.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2034":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain timestamped prices when updates can replace earlier records. Return the price at the greatest timestamp and the minimum and maximum among the current records.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Timestamps and prices are positive integers. Updates may arrive in any timestamp order, including repeated corrections. Queries occur only after at least one update. The latest price is determined by the largest timestamp, not the most recently received update. An extrema query must ignore superseded prices.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Price records arrive with timestamps, a later record may correct an earlier timestamp, and you are asked for the latest, maximum and minimum price.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A heap cannot change an entry in the middle. What if you never change entries, and instead learn to recognise the outdated ones when they reach the top?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Per-key histories with binary search\u003c/p>\u003cp>A dict timestamp -&gt; price is the truth, plus the largest timestamp seen. Push (price, time) onto a min-heap and (-price, time) onto a max-heap on every update. To answer, pop heap tops whose price disagrees with the dict (they were corrected later); the first top that agrees is the answer. Each entry is popped at most once.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(log n) amortised per operation, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep a timestamp-to-price dictionary. Find the current price using the largest timestamp, and scan all current values for minimum or maximum. An update is O(1) expected time and a query costs O(d) for d distinct timestamps, with O(d) storage.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The dictionary is the authoritative current state. Record the largest timestamp ever seen, and push each update into both a min-heap and a max-heap. Heap entries are immutable historical candidates. Before returning an extremum, discard top entries whose stored price differs from the dictionary at that timestamp. A matching top is current; because every update is pushed, every current record has a matching entry in each heap.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Removing stale tops exposes a valid best candidate. If the top is valid, no current price can beat it: its own corresponding heap entry would have appeared earlier. An old entry whose price happens to equal a later correction is harmless, since it is indistinguishable as a candidate for the current value at that timestamp.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The two heaps may contain different stale entries after independent queries. Neither heap is the source of truth, so cleaning one does not require synchronizing the other. The dictionary retains one price for each timestamp regardless of how many corrections were received.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Update time 2 to 9, then time 5 to 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>current \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>, minimum 4, maximum 9\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Correct time 2 to 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>dictionary says {2: 1, 5: 4}; old heap entry 9 is stale\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Ask maximum\u003c/p>\u003cpre>discard stale 9; answer \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>\nlatest timestamp remains 5 despite the correction\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import heapq\n\nclass StockPrice:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.prices = {}\n        self.latest = None\n        self.low = []\n        self.high = []\n\n    def update(self, timestamp, price):\n        self.prices[timestamp] = price\n        if self.latest is None or timestamp &gt; self.latest:\n            self.latest = timestamp\n        heapq.heappush(self.low, (price, timestamp))\n        heapq.heappush(self.high, (-price, timestamp))\n\n    def current(self):\n        return self.prices[self.latest]\n\n    def minimum(self):\n        while self.prices[self.low[0][1]] != self.low[0][0]:\n            heapq.heappop(self.low)\n        return self.low[0][0]\n\n    def maximum(self):\n        while self.prices[self.high[0][1]] != -self.high[0][0]:\n            heapq.heappop(self.high)\n        return -self.high[0][0]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>After u updates, each push or pop costs O(log u); each historical heap entry is popped at most once per heap. Thus operations cost O(log u) amortized, though a single extrema query can discard many entries. Current is O(1). Space is O(u), not merely the number of distinct timestamps, because stale entries can remain buried indefinitely.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Correcting an old timestamp must not change which timestamp is latest.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Store timestamps in heap entries so validity can be checked.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Both heaps need independent stale-entry cleanup.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Querying before the first update is outside the contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use a balanced multiset with explicit deletion to bound storage by live timestamps.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Periodically rebuild both heaps from the dictionary to reclaim stale entries.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:460":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Build a fixed-capacity key-value cache. A successful get and a put of an existing key both increase that key's use count. When inserting a new key into a full cache, evict a key with the smallest count; among tied keys, evict the one used least recently. New keys start with count one, and missing gets return -1.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keys and values are nonnegative, so -1 is reserved for misses. The capacity may be zero. Every successful use increases a count by exactly one, while insertion creates a count of one. This restricted movement lets us maintain the minimum without searching all counts. A missing get is not a use and cannot change the eviction order. Updating an existing value is a use even if the value is unchanged.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Evict the least frequently used key, breaking ties by least recent use, with get and put both O(1).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A heap ordered by (count, last use) is O(log n). Use counts change by exactly one: where does a key move when it is used, and how can the smallest count change?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Frequency buckets with a minimum pointer (LFU)\u003c/p>\u003cp>key -&gt; value, key -&gt; count, and count -&gt; OrderedDict of keys in recency order, plus min_freq. A use moves a key from bucket f to f + 1 and, if bucket f empties and was the minimum, sets min_freq = f + 1. A new key goes to bucket 1 and sets min_freq = 1. Evict popitem(last=False) from bucket min_freq before inserting.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) per operation, O(capacity) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store each key's value, count and last-use sequence number in a dictionary. On eviction scan every key and select the lexicographically smallest pair of count and last-use time. This model is straightforward and useful as an independent test oracle, but a full-cache insertion takes O(c) for capacity c. Alternatively a heap orders candidates in O(log c) time, but does not meet the constant-time goal.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Group keys by frequency, and maintain each group in least-to-most-recent order with an \u003ccode>OrderedDict\u003c/code>. A second dictionary stores each key's value and current frequency. Keep the smallest live frequency in \u003ccode>min_freq\u003c/code>. Because a use moves from f to f + 1, there is no intermediate count to inspect. If the last key in the minimum bucket moves, its new bucket at f + 1 is nonempty and is necessarily the new minimum.\u003c/p>\u003cp>On insertion of a new key, use frequency one and set the minimum to one. If the cache is full, first remove the oldest key from the minimum bucket. We do not need to discover the next minimum after this removal: the immediately following insertion resets it to one. When capacity is zero, return before either step. Within a bucket, only the latest transition into that frequency matters for ordering, since each use moves a key to another bucket.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Initially all maps are empty. Each promotion removes a key from exactly its old bucket and appends it to its new one, updating its recorded frequency. Other keys keep their relative order. Insertion and eviction also preserve the one-bucket-per-key property. Therefore the oldest key in the minimum bucket is exactly the required victim, and successful operations restore every invariant.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The value itself is independent of the bucket position. A replacement changes that value first, then performs precisely the same promotion as a successful lookup. This keeps update and lookup semantics aligned even for repeated updates to the same key.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Capacity 2; put (1, 10), put (2, 20)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>frequency 1, oldest to newest: [1, 2]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Get 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>return \u003cmark>10\u003c/mark>; bucket 1 is [2], bucket 2 is [1]\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Put (3, 30)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>evict 2 from minimum bucket; insert 3 at frequency 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Get 3, then put (4, 40)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>bucket 2 is [1, 3]; evict older 1, not 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The order inside frequency two matters even though the two entries have equal counts.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LFUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.entries = {}\n        self.buckets = {}\n        self.min_freq = 0\n\n    def _touch(self, key):\n        value, freq = self.entries[key]\n        bucket = self.buckets[freq]\n        del bucket[key]\n        if not bucket:\n            del self.buckets[freq]\n            if self.min_freq == freq:\n                self.min_freq = freq + 1\n        new_freq = freq + 1\n        self.buckets.setdefault(new_freq, OrderedDict())[key] = None\n        self.entries[key] = (value, new_freq)\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        if key not in self.entries:\n            return -1\n        value = self.entries[key][0]\n        self._touch(key)\n        return value\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        if self.capacity == 0:\n            return\n        if key in self.entries:\n            _, freq = self.entries[key]\n            self.entries[key] = (value, freq)\n            self._touch(key)\n            return\n        if len(self.entries) == self.capacity:\n            bucket = self.buckets[self.min_freq]\n            victim, _ = bucket.popitem(last=False)\n            del self.entries[victim]\n            if not bucket:\n                del self.buckets[self.min_freq]\n        self.entries[key] = (value, 1)\n        self.buckets.setdefault(1, OrderedDict())[key] = None\n        self.min_freq = 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each operation takes O(1) expected time using hash tables and constant-time ordered-dictionary moves and removals. Each live key is in one entry and one bucket, and empty buckets are deleted, so space is O(c). Python's unbounded integer counts grow in bit length after arbitrarily many operations; the usual complexity model treats these counters and hashes as machine-word operations.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A put on an existing key promotes it rather than resetting its frequency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Emptying the minimum bucket during promotion differs from emptying it during eviction: insertion immediately resets the latter minimum.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Ties require recency within each frequency, not globally oldest creation time.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Capacity zero must not attempt eviction from an empty bucket.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Delete empty buckets to avoid storage growing with past frequencies.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store explicit linked-list nodes instead of relying on \u003ccode>OrderedDict\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change eviction to pure LFU with arbitrary tie-breaking; bucket order then need not track recency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add deletion, but update the minimum carefully because deletion can leave a gap in frequency.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:432":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain positive counts for string keys. Increment adds one, creating an absent key at one. Decrement subtracts one from an existing key and removes it when its count becomes zero. Return any key with the minimum or maximum count, or the empty string if no keys exist.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keys are nonempty strings, and decrements refer to present keys. Counts change by exactly one per update, so the destination count, if already present, must be next to the current count in sorted bucket order. Different keys may have the same count and any tied key is an acceptable answer. Scanning counts or sorting keys on every extremum query would miss the intended constant-time bound.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Counts per key go up and down by one, and you must report a key with the maximum and a key with the minimum count, all in O(1).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A key only ever moves to the bucket of a neighbouring count. What structure keeps buckets in count order and lets you add or remove a bucket next to a known one in O(1)?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Frequency buckets with a minimum pointer (LFU)\u003c/p>\u003cp>A doubly linked list of buckets in increasing count order (with head and tail sentinels), each holding a set of keys, plus key -&gt; bucket. inc and dec move the key to the neighbouring bucket, creating it if the next count is missing and unlinking buckets that become empty. Min is head.next, max is tail.prev.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) per operation, O(number of keys) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain a dictionary of counts. Increment and decrement are expected O(1), but finding either extreme scans all k keys and costs O(k). This simple model is useful for testing because it independently specifies the valid set of answers, including ties.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Maintain a doubly linked list of nonempty buckets ordered by increasing count. Each bucket holds its keys in an \u003ccode>OrderedDict\u003c/code>, and a map points each key directly to its bucket. Two sentinels mark the ends. For an increment, inspect the next bucket: it either represents count f + 1 or a new bucket with that count belongs immediately after the current bucket. Decrement is symmetric, except a count-one key disappears instead of moving to zero.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Creating an absent key is equivalent to moving from the head sentinel to count one. After moving a key, unlink an empty old bucket. The first and last real buckets contain exactly the minimum and maximum counts. Ordered dictionaries provide direct key removal and access to their first key without scanning a bucket; their internal order has no semantic importance here.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The proof is by induction over operations. Initially there are no real buckets and the map is empty. A unit change can skip no integer count between source and destination, so inspecting only the neighboring bucket finds the unique correct destination or position for a new one. Moving the key and deleting any empty source retains strictly increasing, nonempty buckets. The key map then points to its new bucket, or has no entry if the key was removed. Thus the endpoints always answer correctly, even after an extreme bucket vanishes.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This representation does not allocate buckets for missing counts. For example, counts one and ten may be adjacent in the list. An increment from one creates a count-two bucket in between; it must not move directly to count ten. The same reasoning applies in reverse for a decrement.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Increment red twice and blue once\u003c/p>\u003cpre>buckets: 1:{blue} &lt; 2:{red}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Increment blue\u003c/p>\u003cpre>move blue next to red; remove the empty bucket 1\nminimum and maximum may both return \u003cmark>red\u003c/mark> or \u003cmark>blue\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Decrement red twice\u003c/p>\u003cpre>create bucket 1, then remove red completely\nonly blue remains, at count \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Sentinels are not answer buckets and must never be returned as keys.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass _Bucket:\n    def __init__(self, count):\n        self.count = count\n        self.keys = OrderedDict()\n        self.prev = None\n        self.next = None\n\nclass AllOne:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.head = _Bucket(0)\n        self.tail = _Bucket(0)\n        self.head.next = self.tail\n        self.tail.prev = self.head\n        self.where = {}\n\n    def _after(self, node, count):\n        new = _Bucket(count)\n        new.prev, new.next = node, node.next\n        node.next.prev = new\n        node.next = new\n        return new\n\n    def _remove_empty(self, node):\n        if not node.keys:\n            node.prev.next = node.next\n            node.next.prev = node.prev\n\n    def inc(self, key):\n        old = self.where.get(key)\n        prev = old if old is not None else self.head\n        count = old.count + 1 if old is not None else 1\n        target = prev.next\n        if target is self.tail or target.count != count:\n            target = self._after(prev, count)\n        target.keys[key] = None\n        self.where[key] = target\n        if old is not None:\n            del old.keys[key]\n            self._remove_empty(old)\n\n    def dec(self, key):\n        old = self.where[key]\n        if old.count == 1:\n            del self.where[key]\n        else:\n            target = old.prev\n            if target is self.head or target.count != old.count - 1:\n                target = self._after(old.prev, old.count - 1)\n            target.keys[key] = None\n            self.where[key] = target\n        del old.keys[key]\n        self._remove_empty(old)\n\n    def getMaxKey(self):\n        node = self.tail.prev\n        return \"\" if node is self.head else next(iter(node.keys))\n\n    def getMinKey(self):\n        node = self.head.next\n        return \"\" if node is self.tail else next(iter(node.keys))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each operation is O(1) expected time under constant-time hashing and ordered-dictionary operations, with fixed-size keys and count arithmetic. There are at most k nonempty buckets for k live keys, so space is O(k). If key length is unbounded, hashing and comparing strings also costs time in their lengths. Unbounded Python integer counts likewise have nonconstant bit-operation cost.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Returning a particular tied key is not required, and tests must allow all tied extrema.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Removing the final key of a bucket must reconnect both neighboring links.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A decrement to zero deletes the key instead of creating a zero bucket.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Empty structure queries return the empty string, not a sentinel key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Non-unit count changes cannot use this neighbor-only method unchanged.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the extreme count along with a representative key.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Return all tied keys, paying time proportional to the output size.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Allow arbitrary count jumps with a sorted tree, accepting logarithmic updates.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:381":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Design a collection that permits repeated integer values. Insertion returns whether the value was previously absent; removal deletes one occurrence and returns whether one existed. A random operation returns an occurrence uniformly, so a value appearing twice is twice as likely as a value appearing once.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Values may be negative, zero or positive. Random selection is requested only when the collection is nonempty. The collection has no order requirement. A value can occupy several positions, so a single value-to-index mapping is insufficient. Every live occurrence must remain represented, including when a removal and the array's last slot hold equal values.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Like insert, delete and getRandom in O(1), but the same value can be inserted several times and getRandom must weight values by multiplicity.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Keep the array-plus-index-map design. What does the map have to store now that a value can sit at several positions?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Augment a structure with synchronised extra state\u003c/p>\u003cp>A list of values plus a dict value -&gt; set of indices. To remove x, take any index i of x, move the last element (at index n - 1) into slot i, replace n - 1 by i in that element's index set (careful when it is x itself), and pop. random.choice on the list weights by multiplicity automatically.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(1) average per operation, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Store occurrences in a list. Append on insertion and use a random index for sampling, but scan to decide whether a value was present and to find an occurrence to remove. Removing from the middle also shifts a suffix. These operations can take O(n) time for n occurrences, although the list correctly weights random results by multiplicity.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep a dense occurrence array and a dictionary from each value to a set of all indices containing it. Insertion adds the new last index to the appropriate set. To remove an occurrence, take any one of its indices i, move the last value into position i, change the moved value's index set from the old last position to i, and pop the last array entry.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The ordering of updates matters when both values are equal. First remove i from the requested value's set; if i is not the last position, remove the old last index from the last value's set and add i. These operations work even when both sets are the same object. If i is already the last position, no relocation is necessary. Delete a dictionary entry once its set becomes empty. Each occupied position then appears in exactly one index set, namely the set for its array value.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Inductively, insertion establishes this invariant for one new index. Deletion removes the selected occurrence; if another occurrence moves, replacing its index restores the invariant for that one position and no other indices change. Because each occurrence occupies exactly one array slot, choosing a uniform slot returns a value with probability equal to its count divided by the total number of occurrences. Repeated insertions therefore affect probability rather than being silently coalesced.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The set of positions need not be sorted: the identity of the removed occurrence is unobservable. This freedom lets the implementation take an arbitrary position without searching. Tests can inspect all slots directly rather than rely on a probabilistic frequency test that might occasionally fail despite a correct implementation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 5, 5, 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>array [5, 5, 8]; positions 5:{0,1}, 8:{2}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Remove the occurrence of 5 at index 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>move 8 from 2 to 0; array [8, 5]\npositions 8:{0}, 5:{1}\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Insert 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>three slots contain [8, 5, 5]\nprobability of 5 is \u003cmark>2/3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The actual removal may choose another index for 5; either choice preserves the same multiset.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import random\n\nclass RandomizedCollection:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.values = []\n        self.indices = {}\n\n    def insert(self, val):\n        fresh = val not in self.indices\n        self.indices.setdefault(val, set()).add(len(self.values))\n        self.values.append(val)\n        return fresh\n\n    def remove(self, val):\n        if val not in self.indices:\n            return False\n        i = self.indices[val].pop()\n        last_index = len(self.values) - 1\n        last = self.values[last_index]\n        if i != last_index:\n            self.values[i] = last\n            self.indices[last].remove(last_index)\n            self.indices[last].add(i)\n        self.values.pop()\n        if not self.indices[val]:\n            del self.indices[val]\n        return True\n\n    def getRandom(self):\n        return random.choice(self.values)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Insert and remove take O(1) expected amortized time, assuming expected constant-time dictionary and set operations and amortized array append. Random selection is O(1) with a uniform array-index choice. Space is O(n) for n live occurrences, including the index sets. As with ordinary Python hash tables, adversarial collisions can worsen the expected bound.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>When the removed and last values agree, their index sets are the same set; do not delete the mapping before relocation finishes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When removing the last slot itself, do not remove its index twice.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Remove an empty index set so the next insertion correctly reports that the value was absent.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Sampling distinct keys uniformly would produce the wrong distribution.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A random query on an empty collection is outside the stated contract.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Maintain a distinct-value set and sample unique values uniformly instead.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add a count operation returning the size of the value's index set.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Support weighted sampling for arbitrary weights using a different cumulative-weight structure.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2276":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start with no covered integers. Repeatedly add a closed integer interval and report how many distinct integers are covered by at least one interval so far. Overlap and repeated additions must not be counted twice.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each added interval has 1 &lt;= left &lt;= right &lt;= 1,000,000,000. The domain is much too large to allocate one flag per integer. Additions only increase coverage; there is no removal. The inclusive length of a covered interval is right - left + 1. Count queries may occur between arbitrary additions, so the total must remain ready after every update.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Intervals are added one by one, and after any add you may be asked how many distinct integers are covered.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Recounting is too slow. Keep the covered set in a shape where each add changes it locally, and keep the answer up to date as you go.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Augment a structure with synchronised extra state\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep disjoint, sorted intervals and a running total. On add(l, r), find with bisect all intervals that overlap or touch [l, r], subtract their lengths, replace them by their union, and add its length. Each interval is merged away at most once, so merging is amortised; count() returns the stored total.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(log n) amortised search per add (plus the list splice), O(1) per count, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Insert every integer from left through right into a set, and return its size. This is easy to reason about for small domains but an interval can contain a billion integers. Another model stores all intervals and sorts and merges them whenever asked; that avoids per-integer storage but repeats work across queries and can take O(m log m) after m additions.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Use an implicit segment tree for the fixed domain. Each node represents an inclusive interval and stores the number of covered integers in that interval. Its children are created only when an update partially overlaps it. A fully covered node needs no further work: all future additions leave its answer unchanged. If an update fully contains a node's range, mark its count as the range length and discard any children because their details can no longer change the answer.\u003c/p>\u003cp>On partial overlap, split the range at its midpoint, update only intersecting halves, and set the node's count to the sum of its children's counts, treating missing children as zero. The root count is therefore the desired distinct total. The recursion stops at full coverage or a leaf; an intersecting leaf is always fully covered by an integer-bounded update. The implementation uses a count equal to range length as the full-coverage marker, avoiding a separate flag.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Initially the root count is zero. For a fully covered update, assigning the segment length counts each integer exactly once. For a partial update, the disjoint child ranges partition the parent, so summing their correct counts gives the correct parent count. By induction on tree height, the root is correct after every addition. A node already full can return immediately because additions never uncover any integer.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add [2, 4]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>root count becomes 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add [4, 6]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>integer 4 was already covered; root count becomes \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add [2, 6] again\u003c/p>\u003cpre>every covered segment is already full; count remains \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The example shows the logical coverage; the actual tree partitions the entire billion-element domain, not only the displayed numbers.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class _Node:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.count = 0\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\nclass CountIntervals:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.root = _Node()\n        self.limit = 1_000_000_000\n\n    def add(self, left, right):\n        def update(node, lo, hi):\n            if node.count == hi - lo + 1:\n                return\n            if left &lt;= lo and hi &lt;= right:\n                node.count = hi - lo + 1\n                node.left = node.right = None\n                return\n            mid = (lo + hi) // 2\n            if left &lt;= mid:\n                if node.left is None:\n                    node.left = _Node()\n                update(node.left, lo, mid)\n            if right &gt; mid:\n                if node.right is None:\n                    node.right = _Node()\n                update(node.right, mid + 1, hi)\n            node.count = ((node.left.count if node.left else 0) +\n                          (node.right.count if node.right else 0))\n\n        update(self.root, 1, self.limit)\n\n    def count(self):\n        return self.root.count\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let U be the domain size and m the number of additions. At each tree depth at most two nodes are partially intersected; their fully covered siblings need no descent. An add therefore visits O(log U) nodes and takes O(log U) time in the usual unit-cost model; count takes O(1). The tree uses O(m log U) space in the worst case, with O(log U) recursion depth. Fully covered nodes discard descendants, which can require time proportional to the discarded subtree's size for reference-counting garbage collection. Thus in Python the strict per-call bound includes this cleanup; over all updates, total cleanup is bounded by the total number of nodes created, giving O(log U) amortized update time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Closed intervals include both endpoints; a singleton has length one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An overlap must not be added directly to the existing count.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A full node cannot need descendants for future add-only operations.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Updates outside the domain or with left greater than right are outside the contract.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Supporting removals would invalidate the shortcut that discards covered descendants.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Use sorted disjoint intervals and a running total; account for the cost of list shifts.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Coordinate-compress known endpoints offline to reduce the tree domain.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Support removals with coverage counters and retained descendant information.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
